window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][4]={"university_computer_science_en:M4-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M4-L1 – Distributed processing: Queues, workers and AI jobs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor’s / transition: Cloud operations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why a program should not always do its work itself<\/h2>\n<p>A small desktop program can open a file, analyse it and display a\nresult immediately. A simple web service can also operate synchronously:\nthe browser sends a request, the server performs the calculation and\nreturns its response immediately. For a database query or a brief text\nconversion, this is often the most obvious and best architecture.<\/p>\n<p>Things are different when processing takes minutes. A server may need\nto convert a long video, analyse a thousand documents or generate an\nimage using artificial intelligence for a customer. Meanwhile, more\nusers log in. If all the computations took place directly within their\nweb requests, connections could time out, response times could vary\nconsiderably and the web server could become blocked by lengthy tasks.\nMore difficult still: what happens if the responsible process crashes\nduring a job?<\/p>\n<p>This is where <strong>asynchronous processing<\/strong> helps. The\napplication accepts the job, stores it and has a separate process deal\nwith it later. This process can run on the same computer or on another\nmachine. <strong>Distributed processing<\/strong> extends the idea by\ndistributing work among several independent processes or computers when\ndoing so is actually beneficial.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>SYNCHRONOUS\nBrowser ──► Web server ──► Lengthy computation ──► Result\n           (Browser waits until the end)\n\nASYNCHRONOUS\nBrowser ──► Web server ──► Job/queue\n   ▲                           │\n   │                           ▼\n   └──── Status/result ◄── Background process<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The advantage is not automatically a shorter overall computing time,\nbut above all <strong>decoupling<\/strong>: the web application can\nrespond quickly while the demanding work is processed in a controlled\nmanner. However, a new queue means more administration. For very short,\ninfrequent and non-critical tasks, it is unnecessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four basic concepts: Job, queue, worker and scheduler<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>job<\/strong> is a defined unit of work. Examples include\n‘convert file 753’, ‘generate the daily report’ or ‘generate an image\nfrom this prompt’. A well-defined job has a unique identifier, a task\ntype, parameters and a traceable status. Large input files are often\nkept in separate storage; the job then contains only a controlled\nreference to them.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>queue<\/strong> is a buffer for jobs that have not yet been\nexecuted. It separates the time of acceptance from the time of\nprocessing. A queue may be implemented as a database table, as a feature\nof a message broker or as a managed cloud service. Its durability,\nmaximum length and ordering guarantees depend on the particular\nsystem.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>worker<\/strong> is a process that takes on and executes\njobs. One worker might generate PDF files, another process videos and a\nthird run an AI model on a graphics card. Some workers run continuously\nand process many jobs in sequence; others are started afresh for each\njob.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>scheduler<\/strong> determines when a task starts or to\nwhich computer it is assigned. A time-based scheduler, for example,\nstarts an analysis every morning. A resource scheduler finds a computer\nwith sufficient main memory or a suitable graphics card. These two\nscheduling tasks are related but not identical.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                     Job intake\n                         │\n                   ┌─────▼─────┐\n                   │   QUEUE   │\n                   └─────┬─────┘\n                         │\n             ┌───────────┼───────────┐\n             ▼           ▼           ▼\n          Worker A    Worker B    Worker C\n            Text        Video      AI image\n             └───────────┼───────────┘\n                         ▼\n                     Result store<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem does this solve?<\/strong> Different computing\ntasks can be accepted, allocated and executed independently.\n<strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> A local program that carries out a\nsingle short task does not need a specialised resource scheduler.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The lifecycle of a job<\/h2>\n<p>A background job is not simply ‘there’ or ‘gone’. A reliable\napplication requires a state model. The following sketch is a teaching\nexample, not a prescribed standard:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>ACCEPTED ──► WAITING ──► IN_PROGRESS ──► SUCCESSFUL\n                 │              │\n                 │              ├──► RETRY ──► WAITING\n                 │              └──► PERMANENTLY_FAILED\n                 └──► CANCELLED<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>When a request arrives, the user-facing application first checks\npermissions, file sizes and whether the requested processing is\npermissible in principle. It stores a record with a <strong>job ID (job\nidentifier)<\/strong>. A worker reserves the job, carries out the work,\nstores its result and reports completion. The browser can query the\nstatus using the job ID. Alternatively, a service can send a message or\nnotification when the status changes.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>Application Programming Interface (API)<\/strong> does not\nthen have to wait until a lengthy AI computation is finished before\nresponding. It can immediately report: ‘Job 753 accepted’. The result is\nmade available via the API later. However, a job must not be accessible\nmerely by knowing its number: the server must also check whether the\nrequesting user has access to that specific job.<\/p>\n<p>Much can go wrong between ‘accepted’ and ‘executed’. A process may\ncrash, a file may be missing, an external model may fail to respond or a\ncomputation may be cancelled. Status management must make these cases\nvisible rather than leaving a job marked ‘running’ indefinitely.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Message brokers: Intermediaries between programs<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>message broker<\/strong> accepts messages from sending\nprograms and makes them available to suitable recipients. The sending\nprogram is called a <strong>producer<\/strong> and the receiving program\na <strong>consumer<\/strong>. RabbitMQ is an example of such a broker.\nOther products offer components that appear similar, but not necessarily\nthe same delivery and storage rules.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Web application (producer)\n          │\n          ▼\n      Message broker\n          │\n       Work queue\n          │\n       ┌──┴──┐\n       ▼     ▼\n    Worker  Worker\n      1       2<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The broker can buffer messages, route them to consumers and,\ndepending on the method, redeliver them if processing has not been\nacknowledged. This decouples the runtime of the web application from\nthat of the worker. However, it does not mean that messages are\nautomatically stored safely and indefinitely. Persistent storage,\nreplication, retention periods, maximum sizes and acknowledgement\nmechanisms must be selected appropriately.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A broker does not replace the business database.<\/strong> The\ndatabase contains, for example, owners, user permissions, prices, status\nand references to results. The broker distributes the work to be\nperformed. Large videos, model weights or entire image series are not\nusually sent as message payloads; instead, they are placed in suitable\nstorage and only a reference is transmitted.<\/p>\n<p>The disadvantage is the additional operational burden: a broker needs\nresources, updates, access rules, monitoring and a strategy for its own\nfailure. For a few jobs on one server, a properly coordinated database\nqueue may be simpler and more robust.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing work queues, pub/sub and event buses<\/h2>\n<p>In a classical work queue, several workers should <em>share the\npending tasks among themselves<\/em>. This is known as <strong>competing\nconsumers<\/strong>. One worker is selected for each individual delivery.\nThis does not automatically mean that a job can never be executed more\nthan once; retries are possible in the event of failures.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000081_en.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison between a work queue that distributes jobs to competing workers and publish/subscribe, where an event reaches several recipients via an event bus. Failures may require retries and a dead-letter queue.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison between a work queue that\ndistributes jobs to competing workers and publish/subscribe, where an\nevent reaches several recipients via an event bus. Failures may require\nretries and a dead-letter queue.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A queue distributes work to one worker; pub/sub informs several\nindependent recipients. Failures may require retries and a dead-letter\nqueue.<\/p>\n<p>The situation differs with <strong>publish/subscribe\n(pub/sub)<\/strong>: an event is of interest to several independent\nrecipients. For example, the message ‘Order completed’ should reach an\ninvoicing service, a statistics function and a notification service. An\n<strong>event bus<\/strong> routes such events according to types or\nrules. An <strong>event stream<\/strong> is often a continuously readable\nhistory of events retained for a longer period, from which different\nconsumers can read independently.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>QUEUE / DIVISION OF WORK\nJob 1 ──► Queue ──► One of the available workers\n\nPUB/SUB / DISTRIBUTION OF INFORMATION\n&quot;Order completed&quot;\n                │\n                ▼\n             Event bus\n         ┌──────┼──────┐\n         ▼      ▼      ▼\n       Invoice Stats  Email<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A queue therefore typically processes work that needs doing, while\npub/sub makes the same event available to several independent\nrecipients. An event stream often supports repeated processing of\nhistorical events. Real products can combine several patterns; this does\nnot mean that all the terms are interchangeable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> A program with a single background\nworker does not need a separate event bus. Large event architectures\ncreate additional data flows that must be observed, documented and\ntraced when failures occur.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrency, parallelism and distribution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Concurrency<\/strong> means that several tasks can make\nprogress during the same period. For example, a process waits for a\nnetwork response and uses the intervening time to do other work. This is\npossible even with a single processor core.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Parallelism<\/strong> means that several computational tasks\nactually take place simultaneously, for example on different CPU cores.\nThe crucial question is whether the work can sensibly be divided into\nmutually independent parts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Distributed processing<\/strong> uses several independent\ncomputers that cooperate over a network. Each computer can itself work\nin parallel. However, distribution incurs additional overhead through\ndata transfer, synchronisation and possible partial failures.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>CONCURRENT:   One core switches A ── B ── A ── B\nPARALLEL:     Core 1: AAAAAAAA\n              Core 2: BBBBBBBB\nDISTRIBUTED:  Computer 1: AAAAAAAA\n                   Network\n              Computer 2: BBBBBBBB<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An example: a thousand independent images can be distributed among\nseveral workers. If, on the other hand, every computation necessarily\ndepends on the result of the preceding one, multiplying the number of\ncomputers brings little benefit. <strong>Amdahl’s law<\/strong> describes\nthis effect: a serial portion limits the possible speed-up through\nparallelisation. Real systems also incur network and coordination\ncosts.<\/p>\n<p>More servers therefore provide no automatic promise of performance.\nSometimes a powerful single computer is faster, cheaper and easier to\nadminister than a complicated cluster.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CPU and GPU workloads<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>Central Processing Unit (CPU)<\/strong> is suitable for\ngeneral program logic, database work, file processing and a wide variety\nof tasks. A <strong>Graphics Processing Unit (GPU)<\/strong> is\nparticularly efficient at performing many similar computations in\nparallel. AI models often use large matrix operations and can therefore\nbenefit substantially from suitable GPU hardware.<\/p>\n<p>This does not mean that every AI computation requires a GPU or that\nCPU work automatically becomes faster through distribution. A\npreparatory step such as file validation or metadata analysis can run on\na CPU. Demanding image generation may require a GPU and a large amount\nof graphics memory. It is important to know the properties of the task\nand select the appropriate worker.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                   Job intake\n                     │\n              Task classification\n                ┌────┴─────┐\n                ▼          ▼\n          CPU worker    GPU worker\n          Metadata      Image model\n          Parsing       Inference\n                └────┬─────┘\n                     ▼\n                   Result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Inference<\/strong> means running an already trained model on\nnew inputs; <strong>training<\/strong>, by contrast, changes the model\nparameters using data. The two processes can differ considerably in\nmemory requirements, execution time and suitability for distribution.\nTraining large models may require complex coordination of computations\nand memory, which we explore in M5-L3 and M6-L6.<\/p>\n<p>A framework such as <strong>Ray<\/strong> can assign computing jobs to\nappropriate nodes based on logical CPU/GPU requirements. This allocation\ndoes not guarantee that any given task will actually consume only that\namount of physical memory. Actual monitoring and appropriate resource\nlimits are also needed. Some workloads also require particular model\nversions or large files to be locally available on the target computer\nbeforehand.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cost implications:<\/strong> GPU instances may cost money\nwhile idle; on the other hand, starting models and loading weights take\ntime. There is a trade-off between resources that are constantly\navailable and longer waiting times at startup. For manageable\nexperiments of your own, an existing local AI machine may be more\npractical than a permanently rented GPU server.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Short jobs, continuous services and time-based\nscheduling<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>short job<\/strong> ends once a specific task has been\nprocessed, for example creating a thumbnail. A <strong>continuous\nservice<\/strong> remains active to process requests on an ongoing basis,\nfor example a web server or a model server. A <strong>scheduled\njob<\/strong> starts according to a timetable, for example every night. A\npersistent worker can execute many short jobs in sequence; these are\ndifferent levels.<\/p>\n<p>The container orchestration system <strong>Kubernetes<\/strong>\nprovides the <em>Job<\/em> object, which executes specified tasks until\nsuccessful completion, and <em>CronJob<\/em>, which creates jobs\nregularly according to a schedule. However, a CronJob is not\nautomatically a complete business process system; under certain\ncircumstances, multiple jobs can also be created. Retry and concurrency\nrules therefore remain relevant.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Schedule ──► Scheduler ──► &quot;Nightly report&quot; ──► Worker\n\nContinuously running model server ◄── Repeated inference requests<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>For a simple daily report over a local SQLite database, an operating\nsystem scheduler and a small program may suffice. A large container\ncluster becomes interesting only when actual requirements for\nrestarting, allocation, parallelism and changing machines justify\nit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Batch, streaming, low latency and real time<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Batch processing<\/strong> processes a collection of data in\nbatches. An application might analyse a thousand records overnight or\nwork through all new videos in a queue. The advantages are simplicity,\nrepeatability and often good resource utilisation; the disadvantage is\nthat results appear with a delay.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Streaming<\/strong> processes continuously arriving data or\nevents, such as sensor measurements or log events. Ordering, late\nmessages, errors and the state of ongoing analyses play a greater role\nhere.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Low latency<\/strong> means a short response time, for example\nin a chat. This is not equivalent to <strong>hard real time<\/strong>: in\nthat setting, certain actions must be completed within defined\ndeadlines. An ordinary cloud connection and a general-purpose operating\nsystem cannot readily provide such guarantees. Time-critical robotic\ncontrol loops require suitable local hardware and real-time\nmechanisms.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Processing type<\/th>\n<th>Objective<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Typical limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Batch<\/td>\n<td>Process many tasks collectively<\/td>\n<td>Update media catalogue<\/td>\n<td>Results only later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streaming<\/td>\n<td>Analyse continuously arriving data<\/td>\n<td>Sensor and event data<\/td>\n<td>Ordering and state<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low latency<\/td>\n<td>Respond quickly<\/td>\n<td>Interactive chat<\/td>\n<td>Variable waiting time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hard real time<\/td>\n<td>Guarantee a deadline<\/td>\n<td>Safety-critical motor control<\/td>\n<td>Missing a deadline is unacceptable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This distinction becomes particularly important in the robotics\nsection. AI may plan a longer-term task while actual motor control must\nbe executed independently and in a temporally predictable manner.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How large should jobs be?<\/h2>\n<p>A million individual messages for a million tiny image operations can\ncause disproportionately high coordination overhead. A single enormous\njob, on the other hand, may occupy only one worker and require a great\ndeal of work to be repeated after a failure. An intermediate division\noften makes sense, for example batches of several dozen files. The\noptimal batch size depends on computing time, file sizes, retry costs\nand hardware.<\/p>\n<p>Some tasks are independent; others require preparatory work. A\nworkflow can be represented as a <strong>Directed Acyclic Graph\n(DAG)<\/strong>: steps and their dependencies are described as nodes and\narrows, without circular dependencies.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>             Input file\n                 │\n            Read metadata\n                 │\n         ┌───────┴─────────┐\n         ▼                 ▼\n    Generate preview   Recognise speech\n         │                 │\n         └───────┬─────────┘\n                 ▼\n             Build index<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The advantage is that individual substeps can be resumed in a\ntraceable way. The difficulty is that the system must know which\nintermediate results are valid and what must be regenerated after a\npartial failure. Elaborate workflow engines are not essential as long as\nthere are only a few clearly defined steps.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Failures, acknowledgements, retries and idempotence<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000006_en.png\"\nalt=\"Two deliveries of a job and the risk of duplicate charging; comparison without and with atomic idempotency-key verification.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two deliveries of a job and the risk of\nduplicate charging; comparison without and with atomic idempotency-key\nverification.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: Multiple deliveries cannot always be avoided. In\nthe protected arrangement, reservation of the key and the accounting\nentry take effect within suitable atomic processing; external side\neffects such as payments need their own idempotency or reconciliation\nmechanisms.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Jobs can be lost or executed twice at several points. The web\napplication might store a record and crash before sending the message. A\nworker may save the result but fail before reporting completion. A\nredelivered message may then start the work again.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>acknowledgement (ACK)<\/strong> tells the messaging service\nthat a message has been completed according to its delivery model. If\nthe acknowledgement is missing, redelivery can occur. Amazon\n<strong>Simple Queue Service (SQS, a managed queue service)<\/strong>\nuses, among other things, a <em>visibility timeout<\/em>: during this\nperiod, a received message is normally hidden from other consumers. With\nstandard queues, this is not an absolute exactly-once guarantee.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Idempotence<\/strong> describes processing in which repeating\nthe same business job does not produce an unwanted second effect. For\nexample, AI job ID 753 must not be billed twice. Even if image\ngeneration may recompute a result and thereby consume resources, the\nbusiness logic must decide which result applies and which billing entry\nis permitted. <strong>Important when two workers start\nsimultaneously:<\/strong> merely asking ‘Has job 753 already been\ncompleted?’ is not an atomic duplicate-prevention mechanism. Both\nworkers may receive the same answer, ‘no’, before either finishes. For a\nbilling entry that may occur only once, one needs, for example, a unique\njob identifier as a database constraint and an atomically protected\ninsertion, or a suitable transaction or locking mechanism. PostgreSQL\ndocuments <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT<\/code> for this purpose, for\nexample; the specific process must be designed according to the business\nrequirements to prevent not just a second row but also unwanted second\nside effects. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-insert.html\">PostgreSQL –\nINSERT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Worker receives job 753\n        │\n        ▼\nHas job 753 already been completed successfully?\n      │ Yes                   │ No\n      ▼                       ▼\nUse existing result       Computation\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                         Save result\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                      Status + acknowledgement<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>retry<\/strong> is a repeated attempt. A limited retry can\nresolve temporary network failures. Unlimited retries, by contrast,\ncould put an overloaded service under additional strain. Therefore,\nwaiting periods that increase after several failures (<em>backoff<\/em>)\nare often used. Persistently faulty messages can be stored in a\n<strong>dead-letter queue (DLQ)<\/strong>, a holding queue for messages\nthat cannot be processed, where they can be investigated and, if needed,\nresubmitted after correction.<\/p>\n<p>From lesson M3-L5, we are familiar with the <strong>transactional\noutbox pattern<\/strong>. The business modification and the request to\nsend a message later are first recorded in a shared database\ntransaction. A separate process sends the message. This reduces the gap\nbetween a successfully stored job and an unsent queue message. Multiple\ntransmissions must nevertheless be handled at the receiving end.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overload protection and backpressure<\/h2>\n<p>A queue can absorb load spikes, but it is not an infinite buffer. If\nmore jobs arrive over time than all the workers can process together,\nthe backlog grows. Waiting times and possibly storage requirements and\ncosts then increase.<\/p>\n<p>A deliberately simple example: a worker takes an average of 30\nseconds per job and always processes only one at a time. Under idealised\nconditions, it completes two jobs per minute; three identical workers\ncomplete six. If twelve jobs arrive continuously per minute, the backlog\ngrows by approximately six per minute. After ten minutes, assuming no\ninitial backlog or exceptional cases, around 60 additional jobs would be\npending. This is merely a model calculation, not a real performance\npromise.<\/p>\n<p>Countermeasures include <strong>rate limits<\/strong>, maximum queue\nlengths, fair allocation among customers, capacity limits, early\nrejection and <strong>backpressure<\/strong>: an overloaded system\nsignals to earlier stages that they should send less or send more\nslowly. Increasing the number of workers helps only if the database,\nfile storage, external APIs and graphics cards can also cope with the\nadditional load.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Requests ──► Limits / validation\n                    │\n          Capacity available?\n             │           │\n            Yes          No\n             │           └──► Reject/defer in a controlled manner\n             ▼\n          Bounded queue ──► Worker ──► Database / GPU\n                                   ▲\n                             Monitor capacity<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Important metrics include queue length, the age of the oldest\nmessage, time until processing starts, processing duration, error rate,\nnumber of retries and CPU/GPU utilisation. Monitoring, alerting and\nreliability objectives are covered in detail in M4-L2.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical solutions<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"131-lokaler-medienkatalog\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local media catalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A local program is to analyse a thousand video files. An SQLite table\ncan store the state of each file and its analysis jobs. A local\nbackground process takes the next pending tasks and records its results.\nIf two processes are used, reservations and state transitions must be\ncoordinated properly. A messaging service or Kubernetes would often be\nunnecessary here.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Desktop program ──► SQLite job table\n                          │\n                          ▼\n                      Local worker\n                          │\n                          ▼\n                    Media archive / result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The advantages are few dependencies and good controllability. The\ndisadvantage: when the computer is switched off, no work takes place.\nImportant metadata need backups; jobs started during a crash must be\nchecked at the next startup.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"132-kleiner-online-analysedienst\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small online analysis\nservice<\/h3>\n<p>A PHP web application accepts files and analysis jobs. The web\ncomponent manages login, access rights and status queries, while a\nworker carries out the actual computation. Protected file storage holds\nthe input and result. More workers can be added later if necessary.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ──► PHP web application ──► Job database\n                    │\n                    ▼\n                   Queue\n                    │\n                    ▼\n                 CPU worker ──► Result store<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Size limits, file type checks, separation of data by customer,\ndeletion deadlines, status messages, transparent costs and a rule for\nfailed jobs are important. A single well-maintained server may be more\neconomical for low demand than a complex platform. The operator remains\nresponsible for correctness in the application domain, even when renting\nindividual infrastructure services.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"133-ki-auftragsdienst-mit-mehreren-spezialisten\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI job service\nwith several specialists<\/h3>\n<p>A more demanding pipeline might transcribe video material, summarise\nit, generate metadata and create image drafts from it. Different workers\nrequire different hardware. A simple workflow controller tracks which\nsteps are complete and releases dependent jobs only when their\nprerequisites are satisfied.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer ──► Job / authorisation / budget\n                     │\n               Workflow controller\n                     │\n          ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n          ▼          ▼          ▼\n       CPU job  Language model Image model\n                    (CPU/GPU)    (GPU)\n          └──────────┼──────────┘\n                     ▼\n                  Result review\n                     │\n                     ▼\n                Protected storage\n                     │\n                     ▼\n                 Status / billing<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Organisationally, an <strong>AI agent<\/strong> can also be a worker:\nit receives a limited task and uses tools where appropriate. Its\nmodel-driven decisions are not, however, a substitute for fixed\npermission rules, safety checks, cost budgets or human approval for\nrisky actions. How an LLM (Large Language Model) works and how agents\nuse tools are covered in modules M5 and M6.<\/p>\n<p>The development path is not necessarily ‘local → Kubernetes’.\nInstead, it may be: <strong>one local background process → one separate\nworker on the web server → several specialised workers<\/strong>. Every\nadditional step should be justified by measured demand.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Classifying products correctly<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Typical benefit<\/th>\n<th>Often unnecessary when …<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Job table<\/td>\n<td>SQLite/PostgreSQL<\/td>\n<td>Simple job management<\/td>\n<td>The task can be completed directly and\nquickly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message broker<\/td>\n<td>RabbitMQ<\/td>\n<td>Route messages between processes<\/td>\n<td>A single local worker suffices<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed queue<\/td>\n<td>Amazon SQS<\/td>\n<td>Partially outsource queue\ninfrastructure<\/td>\n<td>No ongoing online job processing is\nneeded<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Task framework<\/td>\n<td>Celery<\/td>\n<td>Organise tasks with worker pools<\/td>\n<td>A few simple custom processes suffice<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job orchestration<\/td>\n<td>Kubernetes Jobs/CronJobs<\/td>\n<td>Schedule/monitor many container jobs<\/td>\n<td>A small server suffices<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed computing<\/td>\n<td>Ray<\/td>\n<td>Assign computing tasks to suitable cluster\nnodes<\/td>\n<td>There is no substantial computing need\nsuited to distribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The names are examples, not purchasing or architectural\nprescriptions. A queue is not a complete workflow controller. A\ncontainer orchestrator does not guarantee exactly-once processing of\nbusiness operations. And a broker replaces neither a database nor a\nbackup.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security, maintenance and economic limits<\/h2>\n<p>A distributed system has more access points and potential points of\nfailure. Workers should receive only the permissions needed for their\ntasks. Job messages must not contain unnecessary passwords, secret keys\nor complete personal data. Large inputs belong in protected storage\nareas, not uncontrolled in messages. If external AI services are\ninvolved, it is necessary to check which data are transmitted and how\nthey are processed, stored and deleted. Legal questions depend on the\nindividual case and are not answered here in a binding manner.<\/p>\n<p>A job that has been sent is not yet a successfully executed job. In\naddition to the queue, the database, workers, model servers, external\ninterfaces and result stores must therefore be monitored. The system\nmust be prepared for restarts, lost connections, duplicates and\npersistently faulty jobs. This includes appropriate backups and recovery\ntests, as described in M3-L6.<\/p>\n<p>Costs arise through <strong>CPU and GPU computing time, main memory,\nstorage space, transfers, queue operations, external API calls,\nmonitoring, backups and support<\/strong>. Some systems incur fixed costs\neven while idle; others charge for usage. Faster parallel processing can\ncost more in the short term even when the total amount of computation\nremains the same. Specific provider prices are deliberately not carried\nover from older teaching texts.<\/p>\n<p>The decisive question for every architectural feature remains:\n<em>What real problem does it solve and is its maintenance\nworthwhile?<\/em> A small robust service can be superior to a large,\nelaborate architecture if demand is still low.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>queue<\/strong> buffers jobs; a <strong>worker<\/strong>\nexecutes them; a <strong>broker<\/strong> routes messages; a\n<strong>scheduler<\/strong> plans their execution or resource allocation.\nThese roles can reside on one computer or be distributed across a\nnetwork. Concurrency, parallelism and distribution are different\nconcepts. Batch, streaming, low latency and hard real time satisfy\ndifferent timing requirements.<\/p>\n<p>Reliable job processing requires more than computing power: states,\nunique identifiers, idempotence, limited retries, protection against\noverload and verifiable results. An additional infrastructure component\nmakes sense only if its advantages outweigh the operational\noverhead.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2 –\nDeployment and reliability<\/a>.<\/strong> There we address deployment,\ninfrastructure as code, logs, metrics, monitoring, alerting, rollbacks\nand availability objectives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>Official technical documentation, checked for key statements and\naccessibility on <strong>03.11.2026<\/strong>. The examples illustrate\ngeneral architectural principles; product details and prices must be\nchecked again before specific use.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Microsoft Learn: <em>Queue-Based Load Leveling Pattern<\/em> – queue\nas buffer, backlog, retention and idempotence. <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/queue-based-load-leveling\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/queue-based-load-leveling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Microsoft Learn: <em>Competing Consumers Pattern<\/em> – multiple\nworkers, queues and publish/subscribe. <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/competing-consumers\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/competing-consumers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>RabbitMQ: <em>Tutorial One<\/em> – producers, consumers, brokers and\nmessages. <a\nhref=\"https://www.rabbitmq.com/tutorials/tutorial-one-python\">https://www.rabbitmq.com/tutorials/tutorial-one-python<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Amazon Web Services: <em>Amazon SQS Visibility Timeout<\/em> –\nmessage reservation, repeated delivery. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-visibility-timeout.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-visibility-timeout.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes: <em>Jobs<\/em> – finite and parallel container jobs. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/job/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/job/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes: <em>CronJob<\/em> – regularly scheduled jobs. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/cron-jobs/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/cron-jobs/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Ray: <em>Tasks<\/em> – remote tasks and CPU/GPU requirements. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/ray-core/tasks.html\">https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/ray-core/tasks.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Ray: <em>Key Concepts<\/em> – tasks, actors and distributed resource\nscheduling. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/ray-core/key-concepts.html\">https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/ray-core/key-concepts.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Cross-references:<\/strong> M2-L2 (web applications and APIs),\nM2-L5 (scaling), M3-L5 (distributed correctness), M3-L6 (recovery),\nM4-L2 (monitoring) and M6-L4/M6-L6 (agents and distributed AI).<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M4-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M4-L2 – Deployment and reliability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor’s / transition: Cloud operations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: Programming an application is only the\nbeginning<\/h2>\n<p>A desktop program can be built, tested and started locally. If it\ncrashes, the impact is initially limited to your own computer. A\npublicly available service, however, may be in use when a new software\nversion is installed. In that situation, errors are not merely\ninconvenient: a user might lose a job, receive an invoice twice or be\nunable to access their data for a prolonged period.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Deployment<\/strong> is the process of making a particular\nprogram version available for use in its intended environment. It\ninvolves not only the program files but also settings, required\nservices, access arrangements and, where applicable, database changes.\n<strong>Reliability<\/strong> means that a service functions as expected\nunder defined conditions over a period of time, handles disruptions in a\ncontrolled manner and can be restored after failures.<\/p>\n<p>The two topics belong together: software can run without errors\nduring testing and still fail during release because, for instance, a\nrequired configuration value is missing. Conversely, a well-monitored\nserver is of little use if every release is manual and\nunpredictable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Development ─► Testing ─► Release ─► Running service\n                                          │\n                        Measurement ◄─────┘\n                            │\n                   Detect and fix failures\n                            │\n                     Improve / roll back<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem does this solve?<\/strong> Changes become\ntraceable, disruptions detectable and recovery predictable. <strong>When\nis a large-scale solution unnecessary?<\/strong> A private website that\nchanges infrequently does not require an extensive operations centre. A\ntraceable release process and a usable backup are nevertheless\nworthwhile.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing three things: Application, configuration and\ninfrastructure<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a small online analysis service. Its <strong>application\ncode<\/strong> processes uploaded files. Its\n<strong>configuration<\/strong> determines, for example, how large files\nmay be, which database is accessed and which external services are used.\nIts <strong>infrastructure<\/strong> includes the computer or cloud\nservice, network, data storage and other operational resources.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>       Application code: &quot;What does the program do?&quot;\n                          │\n       Configuration: &quot;How does it run here?&quot;\n                          │\n       Infrastructure: &quot;What does it run on?&quot;\n                          │\n               Running online service<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In a small project, all three may reside on a single virtual server.\nLarger systems separate them more clearly, for example by storing\nprogram code in a container image and operating the database as a\nmanaged service. However, the distinction remains important even with\nsimple web hosting.<\/p>\n<p>A typical mistake is to write credentials, server addresses or secret\nkeys directly into the published program files. Confidential information\nmay then enter the version archive or even appear on a public download\npage. Separate settings and appropriate <strong>secrets\nmanagement<\/strong> for passwords, keys and access tokens are\npreferable. A <em>secret<\/em> is a confidential configuration value;\nmerely converting it to another text format does not protect it. Further\nquestions of access and data protection are discussed in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What exactly is replaced during a release?<\/h2>\n<p>A good release package is <strong>reproducible<\/strong>: it is\npossible to trace which source code and dependencies produced it. In the\ncase of a compiled program, this is an executable file together with the\nrequired libraries. For a PHP application, it may be a defined set of\nfiles; for a containerised application, a <strong>container\nimage<\/strong> as a versioned package of the application and runtime\ncomponents.<\/p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish between the <strong>build<\/strong>\n(creation of the package) and <strong>deployment<\/strong> (installation\nin the target environment). Ideally, a package that has been tested once\nshould reach the production environment without modification. Otherwise,\nthe production version may differ from what was actually tested.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Source code (version X)\n       │\n       ▼\nBuild + tests ──► verified artefact X\n                          │\n                  Approval / deployment\n                          │\n                          ▼\n                  Production environment<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An <strong>artefact<\/strong> here is the finished, distributable\nresult of the build process. The version identifier should be\nunambiguous. For containers, immutable image digests can be used for\nthis purpose, for example; a mere label such as <code>latest<\/code> is\ninsufficient for identifying an exact version in the long term.<\/p>\n<p>Even the most reliable package is of little help if the configuration\nand database state do not match the version. These additional\ndependencies must therefore be part of release planning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Development, test and production environments<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>development environment<\/strong> is used for\nexperimentation and modification. A <strong>test or staging\nenvironment<\/strong> is intended to reproduce important characteristics\nof subsequent operation without affecting real users. The\n<strong>production environment<\/strong> (<em>production<\/em>) is the\napplication actually being offered.<\/p>\n<p>This does not mean that three separate servers must be rented\nimmediately. For very small projects, the test environment can run\nlocally or temporarily. What matters is <strong>what<\/strong> is tested:\ndo the program logic, network settings, database schema, permissions and\nexternal connections work together?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> A test environment is rarely identical\nto production. Data volumes, load, actual browsers, network outages and\npermissions may differ. Tests therefore replace neither monitoring nor\nthe ability to reverse an update in a controlled manner. Conversely, a\ntest environment must not copy actual customer data or production keys\nwithout protection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CI/CD: Testing and deploying changes automatically<\/h2>\n<p>The abbreviation <strong>CI/CD<\/strong> stands for <em>Continuous\nIntegration<\/em> and, depending on usage, <em>Continuous Delivery<\/em>\n(software that is continuously ready for release) or <em>Continuous\nDeployment<\/em> (automated release). The latter two terms are not\nidentical: with delivery, manual approval may precede the production\nstep; with deployment, this step takes place automatically after\nsuccessful checks.<\/p>\n<p>CI ensures that newly merged changes are, for example, built, checked\nfor syntax errors and tested. A defined <strong>workflow<\/strong>\nexecutes individual steps sequentially or in parallel. GitHub Actions is\na particular product that can execute such workflows through jobs on\nso-called runners; the underlying principle is not tied to GitHub.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Change in the version repository\n          │\n          ▼\n        CI: Build ─► Tests ─► Security and quality checks\n                                         │\n                                      Approval\n                                         │\n                            CD: deploy the verified version\n                                         │\n                                 Observe operations<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Such processes help prevent accidentally uploading an old folder or\noverlooking obvious test failures, among other things. They do not\nguarantee freedom from errors: a successful test suite may overlook an\nerror in a rarely used combination.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> A small, rarely updated script\ndoes not need a comprehensive pipeline. A documented process alone —\nwhich version was tested, which files are published, and how can we\nrevert? — is valuable. As releases become frequent or several people\nmake changes, the benefit of automated checks increases\nconsiderably.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Infrastructure as code: A traceable description of the\nenvironment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Infrastructure as Code (IaC)<\/strong> means defining servers,\nnetworks or other resources through versionable, machine-readable\ndescriptions. Instead of repeating a long sequence of clicks for every\nnew installation, you describe the desired target environment and let a\ntool plan or execute the necessary changes.<\/p>\n<p>For example, you specify: ‘There is a private network segment, an\napplication server and a managed data store.’ The tool compares this\ndescription with existing resources. <strong>Terraform<\/strong> is an\nexample of such a platform. Its central approach can be summarised as\n<em>Write – Plan – Apply<\/em>: describe, review planned changes,\nimplement changes.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Versioned infrastructure description\n                 │\n                 ▼\n            Change preview\n                 │\n           Review / approval\n                 │\n                 ▼\n           Cloud API / host\n                 │\n                 ▼\n          Actual infrastructure<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>IaC offers a real advantage for repeatable environments, multiple\nlocations and teamwork. But it also introduces risks: an incorrect\nchange may modify or delete many resources at once. Tools often maintain\na <strong>state<\/strong> (information about the resources they manage),\nwhich itself must be protected and backed up. <strong>IaC is neither\nautomatically a backup nor a guarantee of security.<\/strong> Secrets\nmust not be included in the infrastructure definition or its state file\nwithout appropriate controls.<\/p>\n<p>For a single VPS (Virtual Private Server, a rented virtual server),\nsimple written installation documentation may be more economical than a\ncomplete IaC project.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Container deployment and orchestration<\/h2>\n<p>An application can be packaged as a container image. With a simple\ncontainer runtime or a small Compose setup, an operator can start\nseveral related services. In larger installations,\n<strong>orchestrators<\/strong> such as Kubernetes manage the placement,\nupdating and monitoring of numerous container instances.<\/p>\n<p>In Kubernetes, a <strong>Deployment<\/strong> manages, among other\nthings, the desired state of an application. A release can gradually\nreplace the previously running <strong>Pods<\/strong> (groups of\ncontainers forming execution units) with new ones. Kubernetes provides\n<strong>rolling updates<\/strong> for this purpose. After a failure, it\nis possible under certain conditions to revert to an earlier Deployment\nrevision.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>           Desired version B\n                   │\n                   ▼\n              Orchestrator\n                   │\n     old Pod A ───► new Pod B\n     old Pod A ───► new Pod B\n                   │\n           Load balancing / users<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The technology primarily addresses recurring operational tasks\ninvolving many instances. But it does not turn a faulty application into\na correct one. Kubernetes can restart containers and redirect traffic;\nit does not automatically recognise an invoice calculated incorrectly\naccording to business rules. And reverting a container image does not\nreverse database changes that have already been made.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> For a small online tool,\nconventional web hosting, a VPS or a simple managed platform is often\nsufficient. Kubernetes introduces network, storage, permissions,\nmonitoring and maintenance tasks of its own. Orchestration should solve\nan existing problem, not be an end in itself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Health checks: Is the application running or actually\nusable?<\/h2>\n<p>A process can be active and yet fail to deliver usable results.\nPerhaps it is stuck in an infinite loop, waiting for a failed database\nor has not yet loaded its AI model. Modern operating environments\ntherefore distinguish several types of <strong>health\ncheck<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Liveness:<\/strong> Is the process in a state in which\nrestarting it might help?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Readiness:<\/strong> Can the instance accept new requests\n<em>now<\/em>?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Startup:<\/strong> Has the longer initial phase finished, so\nthat ordinary checks are meaningful?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The Kubernetes project explicitly documents these three checks. The\ndistinction is useful even without Kubernetes.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Service starts ─► Startup check ─► Ready for user requests\n                        │                    │\n                        ▼                    ▼\n                    Not ready yet       Readiness check\n                                             │\n                                  On failure: no new requests\n\nLiveness check: possible restart if a blockage is detected<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A poorly designed health check can itself amplify an outage. If the\ndatabase slows down briefly, causing all web servers to restart\nsimultaneously, additional load is created. Moreover, <strong>a\nsuccessful health check does not prove that the business function is\ncorrect.<\/strong> The response ‘HTTP 200’ may be returned even though\nsome customers can no longer retrieve files. Additional tests and\nmeaningful operational data are needed for this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Monitoring: Avoid operating blindly<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Monitoring<\/strong> means continuously collecting selected\nsignals from a system and responding to unusual conditions. Typical\nquestions are: is the website reachable? How long do responses take? Are\nerrors becoming more frequent? How full is the queue? Is available\nstorage running low?<\/p>\n<p>The obvious temptation is to monitor everything that can be measured.\nHowever, this can easily lead to thousands of figures of no value for\ndecision-making. It is better to begin with the functions important to\nusers. For a file service, this may be successfully accepting and making\nfiles available; for an AI service, completing jobs correctly.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application ─► Measurements ─► Evaluation ─► Alert if needed\n    │                                            │\n    └─────────── User behaviour ◄────────────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Monitoring detects known patterns of failure and breached thresholds.\n<strong>Observability<\/strong> goes further: its aim is to allow\nconclusions about initially unknown causes to be drawn from observable\noutputs. The two terms are related but not synonymous.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logs, metrics and traces – three different\nperspectives<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Logs<\/strong> record individual events associated with a\ntime: ‘Job accepted’, ‘Login denied’, ‘Database connection failed’. They\ntell us <em>what<\/em> a program reported.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Metrics<\/strong> are series of numerical values: requests per\nminute, the proportion of erroneous responses, memory usage, duration of\na calculation. They make changes over time visible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Traces<\/strong> connect related processing steps. A single\nbrowser request may first arrive at the web service, then trigger a\ndatabase query and finally contact a worker. A trace shows where time\nwas spent or an error occurred within this chain. Individual operations\nwithin a trace are called <strong>spans<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User request 847\n       │\n       ├── Web server: 30 ms ──┐\n       ├── Database: 90 ms     ├── Related trace\n       └── External service: ...┘\n\nLogs:    &quot;Timeout at the external service&quot;\nMetric:  &quot;Proportion of failed requests rises&quot;\nTrace:   &quot;Delay originates in the external call&quot;<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The figures are invented solely for illustration.\n<strong>OpenTelemetry<\/strong> is a vendor-neutral framework of tools\nand specifications for collecting and transmitting such telemetry data.\nIt is not automatically the database in which the data is stored\npermanently, nor is it a complete alerting centre.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational implication:<\/strong> Logs and traces may contain\npersonal data, file names, requests or secrets. Data minimisation,\naccess protection and an appropriate retention period are therefore\nnecessary. Detailed logging of every request may also create high\nstorage and transmission costs.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000013_en.png\"\nalt=\"Logs show individual events, metrics show figures over time, traces show related processing steps\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Logs show individual events, metrics show\nfigures over time, traces show related processing steps<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three perspectives on the same operations: logs are\nevents, metrics are time series, and traces connect the spans of a call.\nAll illustrated values are hypothetical; sensitive information must not\nenter telemetry data unfiltered.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Alerting: An alert must make a response possible<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Alerting<\/strong> means triggering a notification when a\nrelevant condition occurs. A useful alert might read: ‘For ten minutes,\nmany users have been unable to retrieve their jobs.’ A less useful one\nwould read: ‘CPU usage briefly exceeded 65 per cent’ — without reference\nto harm or a necessary intervention.<\/p>\n<p>Alerts should reach the person responsible for operations and make it\npossible to identify an action. Otherwise, <strong>alert\nfatigue<\/strong> develops: when many meaningless notifications arrive\nevery day, important warnings are overlooked.<\/p>\n<p>A simple hierarchy helps:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Information:<\/strong> An unusual condition is logged; no\nimmediate action required.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Warning:<\/strong> A threshold is approaching; review\ncapacity or the error trend later.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Critical:<\/strong> An essential user workflow is disrupted;\nact promptly.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For a small private service, an automated availability check with an\nemail notification may initially be enough. Anyone operating a paid\nservice with binding commitments, however, needs a realistic way to\nhandle disruptions outside normal development hours. A notification\nalone does not repair an application.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Updates without unnecessary disruption: Four\nstrategies<\/h2>\n<p>The simplest approach is called <strong>Recreate<\/strong>: stop the\nold application and start the new one. There is usually a brief\ninterruption. For smaller services with acceptable maintenance windows,\nthis may be entirely appropriate.<\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>rolling update<\/strong>, instances are replaced\ngradually. One prerequisite is that old and new versions can work\ntogether temporarily. A <strong>blue-green deployment<\/strong> maintains\ntwo separate environments: the new version is first tested on the\nnon-public environment, then traffic is switched. In a <strong>canary\nrelease<\/strong>, only a small proportion of users or requests receive\nthe new version initially, before the release is expanded.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>RECREATE:      [Version A] ──Stop──► [Version B]\nROLLING:       [A A A] ─► [A A B] ─► [A B B] ─► [B B B]\nBLUE-GREEN:    Users ─► [Blue / A]    [Green / B ready]\n                               └── Switch ─► [Green / B]\nCANARY:        95 % to A  +  5 % to B (example values only)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>These methods reduce some release risks but do not prove continuous\navailability. Running several versions simultaneously may incur\nadditional server costs. Canary distributions need enough actual\nrequests to produce meaningful error statistics. For an application with\nfew users, a deliberate maintenance window is sometimes preferable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Feature flags: Enabling functions independently of the\nprogram package<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>feature flag<\/strong> allows a function to be included in\nthe software without immediately making it available to all users. For\nexample, a new export function may initially be visible only to internal\ntesters or selected customers. The flag controls behaviour at runtime\nand can often be changed more quickly than a complete deployment.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Same program version\n       │\n       ├── Flag OFF ─► Existing workflow\n       └── Flag ON  ─► New function<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Flags are particularly useful when release and activation need to be\nseparated. However, they offer no magical way back: if the new function\nhas already modified data or triggered chargeable actions, turning off a\nflag does not undo those consequences.<\/p>\n<p>Furthermore, each flag adds possible combinations that must be tested\nand later tidied up. A single small web service therefore does not\nautomatically need a professional feature flag system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rollback: Why an old program does not automatically mean\nold data<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Rollback<\/strong> means reversing a change to return to a\nprevious working state. For a simple website, redeploying the previous\nfiles may be sufficient. Databases make things more complicated: a new\nversion might have renamed a column, converted data to a different\nformat or processed new jobs.<\/p>\n<p>The old version may then suddenly be unable to work with the new\ndata. Restoring the entire data set to an earlier point in time would in\nturn be dangerous, because customer actions performed in the meantime\nwould be lost.<\/p>\n<p>One common approach is a <strong>backwards-compatible database\nmigration<\/strong>: first, the data structure is extended so that both\nold and new program versions can work with it; obsolete parts are\nremoved only at a later stage. This basic idea is often called\n<em>Expand and Contract<\/em>. It increases maintenance effort in the\nshort term but can facilitate safe, gradual releases.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1. Extend the database schema (old version keeps running)\n2. Deploy the new program version\n3. Switch data / user access in a controlled manner\n4. Remove obsolete fields and paths only later<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Distinction from M3-L6:<\/strong> A rollback is not a regular\ndata backup. Backups and recovery need a strategy of their own. Data\nconsistency must be checked even after an apparently successful\nrollback.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measuring reliability: SLI, SLO and SLA<\/h2>\n<p>When a service is supposed to be ‘reliable’, the question is: what\ndoes this mean in concrete terms? Three terms help:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Service Level Indicator (SLI):<\/strong> A measurable\nquantity, such as the proportion of successful uploads or a service’s\nresponse time.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Service Level Objective (SLO):<\/strong> A deliberately\ndefined target for an indicator, for example ‘at least 99.5 per cent of\nuploads successfully completed within a month’; this value is merely an\nexample.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):<\/strong> An agreement with\ncustomers on promised services and any consequences of failing to meet\nthem.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>What do we measure? SLI: successful uploads / all uploads\nWhat quality do we want? SLO: e.g. 99,5 % in a calendar month\nWhat do we promise? SLA: contractual commitment with conditions<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An SLO is not automatically a legally guaranteed commitment. A\npurported ‘99.9 per cent availability’ rate is also of little\nsignificance without a precise measurement definition: does a broken\nupload function count as an outage even if the home page remains\nreachable? Which user regions and time period are included? Are\nannounced maintenance windows taken into account?<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>error budget<\/strong> is the calculated deviation\npermitted within the framework of an SLO. It helps weigh up whether new\nfeatures should be released quickly or whether stabilisation should take\npriority. It is not a credit balance with which errors should\ndeliberately be ‘spent’. Google describes this separation of indicators,\nobjectives and agreements in its materials on Site Reliability\nEngineering (SRE, systematic reliability engineering).<\/p>\n<p>For a small free service, a few realistic internal goals may suffice\ninitially. Binding SLA commitments without staff, an emergency plan and\nreliable measurements, on the other hand, may have substantial support\nand liability implications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>One server or multiple availability zones?<\/h2>\n<p>A single rented server may be reliable enough for many applications —\nparticularly if an operator can tolerate downtime and has a tested\nrecovery process. Nevertheless, it has an obvious <strong>single point\nof failure<\/strong>: if the machine or its location fails, the service\nmay become unreachable.<\/p>\n<p>Multi-zone operation distributes suitable resources across separate\n<strong>availability zones<\/strong> within one cloud region. This may\nmitigate the effects of certain infrastructure failures. A prerequisite,\nhowever, is that the database, load balancing, network access and\ndependent services also match the desired fault tolerance. Two web\nservers in separate zones are of little help if both need the same\nfailed database.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>SMALL SERVICE\nUsers ─► One VPS ─► Database + backup elsewhere\n\nMULTI-ZONE OPERATION (simplified)\n                          ┌──► Zone A: Web + data access\nUsers ─► Load balancing ──┤\n                          └──► Zone B: Web + data access\n                                      │\n                               Coordinated data storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Multi-zone operation also means additional network connections,\nreplication where applicable, failover tests and often higher fixed\ncosts. <strong>Availability<\/strong> and <strong>data\ndurability<\/strong> are different objectives: a service may continue\nhandling requests during a failure while still losing individual data\nitems — or retain all data while remaining unreachable for hours. The\ndatabase aspects were discussed in M3-L2 and M3-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How incident handling is organised<\/h2>\n<p>A technical failure requires not only tools but also decisions. A\nmanageable incident process looks like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Incident detected\n      │\n      ▼\nIdentify affected functions and users\n      │\n      ▼\nLimit damage (e.g. pause new jobs)\n      │\n      ▼\nRestore / activate a safe state\n      │\n      ▼\nCheck data and business workflows\n      │\n      ▼\nDocument causes and improve protection<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Anyone operating a service should know where relevant logs are\nlocated, who has access, which changes were released most recently and\nwhich recovery measures actually work. With several people involved,\nclear allocation of responsibilities becomes important. Without these\narrangements, an apparently small disruption may persist for a long\ntime.<\/p>\n<p>This does not mean that an individual developer needs an incident\ncommand centre staffed around the clock. But they should not promise\ncustomers availability that they cannot realistically guarantee without\nan appropriate organisation. Particularly with paid jobs, clear status\nmessages and a practical support channel also contribute to\nreliability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three application examples: From simple to distributed<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-private-documentation-website\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A: Private\ndocumentation website<\/h3>\n<p>An information website that changes infrequently is operated by a\nconventional web hosting provider. Changes are tested locally and\nuploaded as clearly named versions. The domain and TLS certificate are\nmonitored; the contents are also stored in an independent archive. If a\nchange fails, the previous set of files is published again.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Appropriate:<\/strong> Little infrastructure, simple control,\nlow maintenance. <strong>Excessive:<\/strong> Kubernetes, multi-zone\ndatabase clusters, tracing between ten microservices. <strong>Residual\nrisk:<\/strong> Provider outage and delays in manual recovery.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-small-paid-online-analysis-tool\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B: Small paid\nonline analysis tool<\/h3>\n<p>The application consists of a web interface, a backend and a\ndatabase. Git manages the source code. Automated basic tests run before\nreleases. The operator checks whether a new version requires database\nmigrations and subsequently monitors error rates, response times and\nsuccessful jobs.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Users ─► Web/API service ─► Database\n                    │\n                    └──► Monitoring / alert\nDevelopment ─► Testing ─► Versioned deployment<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Appropriate:<\/strong> Simple CI, a few measurements, recovery\ntest, clear maintenance information. <strong>Excessive:<\/strong> Two\nadditional cloud regions and an elaborate canary process for a few\nrequests per day. <strong>Operational implications:<\/strong> Security\nupdates, user permissions, backups, customer communication and\nsupport.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-distributed-ai-processing-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C:\nDistributed AI processing service<\/h3>\n<p>A web service accepts jobs and a queue distributes them to workers\nwith suitable hardware. Some of these workers load large models into\ngraphics memory and therefore take longer to start. A new version should\naccept jobs only once the model is actually available. In the event of a\nfailure, the same job must not inadvertently be executed and charged\ntwice.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Users ─► API ─► Job database\n                 │\n                 ▼\n               Queue\n          ┌──────┴──────┐\n          ▼             ▼\n     GPU worker A  GPU worker B\n          └──────┬──────┘\n                 ▼\n           Result store\n                 │\n             Status + logs + metrics + traces<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Queue length, job waiting time, failed jobs, model loading failures\nand costs per successful job are often more informative here than just\nthe web server’s CPU usage. A worker restart must be accompanied by a\nsafe resumption or deliberate repetition of a job. With expensive GPU\nresources, a further crucial question may be whether a second worker\nremains available continuously or starts only when needed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Appropriate:<\/strong> Clear job records, idempotency, health\nchecks, traceable versions and cost alerts. <strong>Excessive:<\/strong>\nA global Kubernetes fleet if the actual workload can be processed\nreliably and economically on a single local GPU.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison: Which level of operation suits which need?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operating model<\/th>\n<th>Typical deployment<\/th>\n<th>Typical monitoring<\/th>\n<th>Additional effort<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Local tool<\/td>\n<td>Manually approved program version<\/td>\n<td>Functional test, version archive<\/td>\n<td>Updates and local data backup<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small static website<\/td>\n<td>Version-specific file upload<\/td>\n<td>Availability, certificate, content<\/td>\n<td>Hosting and recovery<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small VPS web service<\/td>\n<td>Controlled deployment, CI if appropriate<\/td>\n<td>Logs, error rate, backups<\/td>\n<td>Operating system, security, support<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed application platform<\/td>\n<td>Provider-supported deployment<\/td>\n<td>Application metrics, external dependencies<\/td>\n<td>Usage fees, platform limitations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed container platform<\/td>\n<td>Orchestration, rolling/canary releases<\/td>\n<td>Traces, SLOs, multi-level alerts<\/td>\n<td>Many infrastructure and data dependencies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This table is not a rigid progression. An application does not have\nto move to Kubernetes at some point simply because user numbers grow.\nPerformance requirements, the consequences of outages, data protection,\noperating hours and available staffing capacity together determine the\narchitecture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M4-L3<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Deployment<\/strong> is the controlled release of a particular\nprogram version together with suitable settings and dependencies. CI/CD\nand Infrastructure as Code can improve repeatability if their additional\ncomplexity is justified. Containers and orchestrators facilitate\noperating many instances but replace neither business logic nor database\ncorrectness.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reliability<\/strong> does not arise simply from having more\nservers. It requires meaningful tests, good health checks, logs, metrics\nand traces, clear alerts, a realistic recovery process and honest\nservice level objectives. Rollbacks, blue-green and canary methods can\nreduce release risks but are no substitute for backups or the testing of\ndata migrations.<\/p>\n<p>The practical guiding question is: <strong>Which disruption would\nreally cause problems for users — and which simple, verifiable measure\nprevents or limits precisely that disruption?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Next comes <strong>M4-L3 – Security, data protection and\nresponsibility<\/strong>. There we examine identity and permissions\nmanagement, secrets management, attacks on publicly accessible\ninterfaces and responsibility for personal data, among other topics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Verification date: 03.11.2026.<\/strong> The following project\nand vendor pages are sources for the technical principles described.\nSome literature on SLOs is older but remains a fundamental definition of\nthe concepts. Provider prices and specific product commitments are not\npart of this lesson.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>GitHub Docs: <em>Understanding GitHub Actions<\/em> – CI/CD\nworkflows, jobs, runners and automation. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/understand-github-actions\">https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/understand-github-actions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>HashiCorp Developer: <em>What is Terraform?<\/em> – IaC, versioned\ninfrastructure, Write/Plan/Apply and state. <a\nhref=\"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/intro\">https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/intro<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes Docs: <em>Deployments<\/em> – rolling updates, revisions\nand rollback. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes Docs: <em>Configure Liveness, Readiness and Startup\nProbes<\/em> – different types of health check. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OpenTelemetry: <em>Observability Primer<\/em> – logs, metrics, traces\nand observability. <a\nhref=\"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/observability-primer/\">https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/observability-primer/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OpenTelemetry: <em>What is OpenTelemetry?<\/em> – vendor-neutral\ncollection and transmission of telemetry data. <a\nhref=\"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/what-is-opentelemetry/\">https://opentelemetry.io/docs/what-is-opentelemetry/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Google: <em>Site Reliability Engineering – Service Level\nObjectives<\/em> – SLI, SLO, SLA and error budget. <a\nhref=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\">https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Microsoft Learn: <em>Safe deployment practices<\/em> – canary,\nblue-green and gradual release. <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/operational-excellence/safe-deployments\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/operational-excellence/safe-deployments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes Docs: <em>Secrets<\/em> – confidential values and\nlimitations of simple encoding. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M4-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M4-L3 – Security, data protection and responsibility<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor’s / transition: Cloud operations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: An accessible service also presents an\nattack surface<\/h2>\n<p>In the previous lessons, we made applications accessible over the\ninternet, distributed them across several servers and prepared them for\nreliable operation. An additional task now arises: not everyone who can\nreach the service may use it without restrictions. And even an\nauthorised user is not normally permitted to access other users’\ndata.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Information security<\/strong> aims to protect information and\nsystems against unauthorised access, unwanted modification and outages.\nThe three frequently cited fundamental objectives are\n<strong>confidentiality<\/strong> (not everyone may read the\ninformation), <strong>integrity<\/strong> (data and processes should not\nbe altered without detection) and <strong>availability<\/strong>\n(authorised users should be able to use a service). <strong>Data\nprotection<\/strong> additionally asks whether the processing of\n<em>personal data<\/em> is justified, transparent, limited to a purpose\nand appropriate. A technically secure service may nevertheless violate\ndata protection rules if it collects more data than necessary.<\/p>\n<p>An example: a customer’s analysis is correctly transmitted over an\nencrypted connection. Nevertheless, a programming error might allow a\nlogged-in customer to download another customer’s file by changing a job\nnumber. The encryption works; the <strong>authorisation check<\/strong>\nis missing.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Public internet\n        │\n        ▼\n    Web service / API\n        │\n  Who is accessing it? ──► Verify identity\n        │\n  What may they do?    ──► Check permission for this exact object\n        │\n        ▼\n   Business function ──► Protected database\n        │\n        └──────────────► Traceable security events<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem do security measures solve?<\/strong> They limit\naccess, misuse, data loss and operational disruptions. <strong>When is a\nlarge security platform excessive?<\/strong> For a purely local, offline\ntool that does not handle other people’s data. Nevertheless, backups and\nbasic system hygiene remain important even there. The appropriate level\nof protection depends on the potential harm, not on how modern the\nsystem is supposed to look.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Understanding the risk first: Assets and threat models<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>threat model<\/strong> is a structured examination of what\nneeds protection, who might cause harm and how, and where the relevant\ninterfaces are located. Initially, this does not require complicated\nsoftware.<\/p>\n<p>For a small online service, the assets might include customer files,\naccounts, API keys, financial balances and the application’s\navailability. Risks range from accidental misconfiguration and stolen\ncredentials to automated abuse. Not every disruption is a deliberate\nattack: an incorrectly assigned database permission or a publicly\naccessible storage bucket can have equally serious consequences.<\/p>\n<p>It helps to think in terms of <em>trust boundaries<\/em>. Between a\npublic browser and a server, transmitted data must never be assumed\ncorrect merely because the interface has already checked them. Between\nthe application server and the database, permissions must be restricted.\nBetween your own AI worker and an external model provider, a further\nboundary involving its own data transfer may arise.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[Unknown browser] ─┬─ Trust boundary ─► [Own application]\n                   │                           │\n          Validate inputs              Check rights and data\n                                               │\n                                      Database trust boundary\n                                               │\n                                      [Customer data / jobs]\n                                               │\n                                   Possibly another external boundary\n                                               ▼\n                                     [External service provider]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The security question is not only ‘Can someone break in?’ but also\n‘What happens if a single error occurs, and how far do its consequences\nextend?’ Consistently limiting permissions ensures that a compromised\ncomponent does not automatically control the entire system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Authentication and authorisation are two different\ntasks<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Authentication<\/strong> means that a system checks whether\nsomeone can prove their claimed identity. <strong>Authorisation<\/strong>\nmeans that the system decides what this identity is permitted to do. The\ntwo steps often occur in sequence, but they must not be confused.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000071_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic access process: verify identity, then check permission for an order. A valid login does not automatically permit access to other people’s orders.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic access process: verify\nidentity, then check permission for an order. A valid login does not\nautomatically permit access to other people’s orders.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Authentication answers ‘Who?’; authorisation determines ‘Is this\nperson allowed to do that?’<\/p>\n<p>A user may be correctly logged in and still lack permission to open\nsomeone else’s order. Conversely, a public article may deliberately be\nreadable without a login. The two questions are independent.<\/p>\n<p>The abbreviation <strong>IAM<\/strong> (<em>Identity and Access\nManagement<\/em>) refers to the management of identities and access\nrights. It includes user accounts, machine identities, role assignments,\nlogin mechanisms and the withdrawal of rights that are no longer\nneeded.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Least privilege<\/strong>, or the <em>principle of least\nprivilege<\/em>, means that each identity receives only the access it\nactually requires, preferably for no longer than necessary. A web\nprocess that must read customer files therefore does not automatically\nneed permission to delete all database tables. An automated image worker\nshould not have the same extensive administrator key as the\noperator.<\/p>\n<p>A common method is <strong>role-based access control<\/strong> (RBAC):\nroles such as ‘customer’, ‘support’ or ‘administrator’ receive different\npermissions. With <strong>attribute-based access control<\/strong>\n(ABAC), additional characteristics such as ownership, project membership\nor data classification may be decisive.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Access scenario<\/th>\n<th>Identity verification<\/th>\n<th>Additional authorisation check required<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer reads their own job<\/td>\n<td>Logged-in session or valid access token<\/td>\n<td>Does this exact job belong to the customer?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employee handles a support case<\/td>\n<td>Employee identity<\/td>\n<td>Is access to this case and this data category permitted?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI worker loads an input file<\/td>\n<td>Machine identity<\/td>\n<td>Is this exact file authorised for this exact job?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operator changes server settings<\/td>\n<td>Strongly protected administrator account<\/td>\n<td>Is the appropriate operational permission present?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A particularly important technical consequence is that the check must\ntake place <strong>in the backend for every relevant action<\/strong>. A\nhidden button or a file number that is not displayed is not access\nprotection. The Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP)\ncalls such object-level authorisation failures <em>Broken Object Level\nAuthorization<\/em> in its API security overview.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Passwords, MFA and passkeys<\/h2>\n<p>A password is a secret used to log in, either alone or together with\nother evidence. An insecure login system can undermine all the other\nmeasures even when the database is otherwise well protected.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Multi-factor authentication<\/strong> (MFA) combines different\ntypes of evidence, for example knowledge (a password) and possession (a\ndevice or security key). Two passwords, by contrast, are not\nautomatically two independent factors. MFA reduces the risk posed by\nstolen passwords but does not guarantee complete protection against\nevery form of deception or account takeover.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Passkeys<\/strong> typically use the FIDO2/WebAuthn standards\nand cryptographic key pairs. With an appropriate implementation, users\ncan log in without a reusable website password; the private key does not\nleave the corresponding authenticator in the usual authentication\nprocess. This can be particularly resistant to conventional phishing\nsites. The precise design can vary between devices, synchronisation\nmechanisms and providers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>NIST SP 800-63B-4<\/strong>, published in 2025 (<em>National\nInstitute of Standards and Technology, Special Publication<\/em>, a\nguideline on authentication), is a current technical reference. Its\nrequirements apply directly to the scope defined in the guideline, not\nuniversally as a law for every private web application. Nevertheless, it\nprovides useful guidance on strong, user-friendly authentication\nmethods.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User ─► Login method ─► Verified identity\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                       Short-lived session\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                      Authorise each action<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Why not every project should develop its own login\nsystem:<\/strong> Secure authentication also requires account recovery,\nprotection against automated attempts, session expiry, revocation of\nlost access credentials and handling of stolen credentials. An\nestablished authentication solution may help avoid errors, but it also\ncreates operational and provider dependencies. A public documentation\nproject without user accounts may not need a login at all.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Storing passwords: Hash instead of plaintext<\/h2>\n<p>If a service processes passwords, they must not end up in a database\nas plaintext. Simple reversible encryption is also not normally the\ncorrect underlying principle for password verification.<\/p>\n<p>Instead, a deliberately computationally expensive <strong>hash\nalgorithm<\/strong> specifically suited to passwords is used. A\n<em>hash<\/em> here is a one-way verification value; a random\n<strong>salt<\/strong> (an individual additional value) and suitable cost\nparameters make large-scale guessing more difficult. The server then\nstores the values required for verification but not the original\npassword. Modern methods include <strong>Argon2id<\/strong>,\n<strong>scrypt<\/strong> and, for certain requirements,\n<strong>PBKDF2<\/strong> (<em>Password-Based Key Derivation Function\n2<\/em>). An ordinary, very fast SHA-256 hash alone is unsuitable for\npassword storage.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>New password ─► Salt + suitable password hash ─► Store verification value\n\nLogin password ─► Same verification procedure ─► Compare ─► Yes / no<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The precise algorithm and its parameters must suit the runtime\nenvironment, expected load and current recommendations. Even a correctly\nstored hash is of no help if the password appears in a log or error\nmessage beforehand. The OWASP password storage guideline provides\nspecific technical recommendations that can be reviewed regularly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sessions, cookies and access tokens<\/h2>\n<p>After a login, a service must be able to recognise the user during\nlater requests. This often takes place through a\n<strong>session<\/strong> and a <strong>cookie<\/strong> transmitted by\nthe browser. Ideally, the cookie should contain only a session\nidentifier that is difficult to guess, while the actual authorisation\ninformation is kept under control on the server side.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>token<\/strong> is digital evidence of a particular access\nright. API access tokens may have permissions and validity periods. The\nformat alone does not prove that a token is secure: it must be verified\nfor authenticity, restricted, stored securely, appropriately revocable\nor short-lived, and associated with the correct recipient.<\/p>\n<p>A typical browser session needs further protective rules, for example\n<code>Secure<\/code> for encrypted transmission only and\n<code>HttpOnly<\/code> so that browser scripts cannot directly read a\ncookie. <code>SameSite<\/code> can make certain cross-site request\nscenarios more difficult; it does not replace every necessary measure\nagainst <strong>Cross-Site Request Forgery<\/strong> (CSRF, forced\nrequests through other websites). The appropriate settings depend on the\ntype of application.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> A public, entirely static document\nwithout user functionality requires neither sessions nor personalised\naccess tokens. Every additional login mechanism also introduces new\nrisks and maintenance burdens.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Machine identities, secrets and key management<\/h2>\n<p>Humans are not the only ones who log in. An application accesses a\ndatabase, a worker loads files and a service uses an external model\nthrough a programming interface. This often requires\n<strong>secrets<\/strong> — confidential values such as database\npasswords, API keys or private cryptographic keys.<\/p>\n<p>Such values should not appear in public source code, Git repositories\nor permanently distributed container images. Base64 encoding is not\nencryption either. A <strong>secret manager<\/strong> or <em>key\nvault<\/em> can centrally manage storage, permissions, traceability and\nreplacement of secrets. For small installations, restrictively protected\nconfiguration files may suffice if the risk and environment permit it.\nEnvironment variables are frequently used, but they too can be exposed\nthrough careless diagnostics or process inspection.<\/p>\n<p>Important terms:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>API key:<\/strong> A secret or identifier for programmatic\naccess; depending on the system, it may represent authentication alone\nor be combined with other evidence.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Access token:<\/strong> Evidence of access limited by time or\npurpose.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Rotation:<\/strong> Scheduled or incident-related replacement\nof a secret, for example after suspected compromise.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Key management:<\/strong> Generating, storing, using,\nrenewing and destroying cryptographic keys.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application ─► Restricted machine identity\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                   Secrets management\n                      ├── DB access only for this application\n                      └── API access only for this external service<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The principle is that not all services should use the same master\nkey. The effects of a single failure can then at least be confined to a\nlimited area. However, this requires documentation and regular review of\npermissions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Encryption: In transit and at rest<\/h2>\n<p><strong>TLS<\/strong> (<em>Transport Layer Security<\/em>, transport\nencryption) typically protects confidentiality and integrity during\ntransmission when connections are established correctly.\n<strong>HTTPS<\/strong> combines the HTTP web protocol with TLS. This is\nindispensable for login and customer data, but it does not protect\nagainst every security vulnerability <em>within<\/em> the recipient\nsystem.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Encryption at rest<\/strong> protects stored contents against\ncertain attacks, such as access to lost storage devices or unauthorised\nstorage images. Whether it also helps against a compromised, running\napplication server strongly depends on where the key is located and who\nmay use it. If the application and the attacker have the same access to\nthe key, a substantial part of the risk remains.<\/p>\n<p><strong>End-to-end encryption<\/strong> is a stronger, independent\narchitectural principle: only the intended endpoints should be able to\nread the plaintext. This is not automatically the case merely because\ndata are transmitted to a cloud application via HTTPS. If a server must\nanalyse customer files, the application usually requires access to their\ncontents; this has consequences for its data protection and security\narchitecture.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser == TLS ==► Application ─► Database with storage encryption\n                         │                      │\n                  Possible plaintext here    Key access<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Which technology solves which problem?<\/strong> TLS protects\nthe transmission route, disk encryption protects against certain storage\nlosses, permissions management controls permitted access, and backups\nenable recovery. No single measure replaces the others.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security updates, backups, monitoring and incident\nresponse<\/h2>\n<p>Security is not a one-off installation. Operating systems, libraries,\napplication dependencies and devices need updates. A project’s\ndependencies — for example a PHP library or a container base image — can\nalso become vulnerable. An update must therefore be tested and applied\npromptly without carelessly damaging production data.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Backups<\/strong> are explicitly part of security,\nparticularly availability. However, a backup archive is useful only if\nit is protected, complete and recoverable. The details of recovery\nobjectives and testing were discussed in M3-L6. Particularly important:\na backup may also contain personal data and credentials; its own\nretention must be taken into account.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security monitoring<\/strong> should detect unusual login\nactivity, large numbers of failed authorisation checks or unexpectedly\nhigh levels of API calls. At the same time, logs must not themselves\nbecome an unprotected collection of sensitive customer data. Instead of\ncomplete file contents or password values, event type, time, technical\nidentifier and a limited error description are often sufficient.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Incident response<\/strong> comprises detection, containment,\nassessment, remediation and follow-up. If, for example, an API key has\nbecome public, deleting the affected file later is not enough. The key\nmust be treated as compromised and revoked or replaced; access and\npossible consequences must be investigated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Public APIs: Data leakage, spam and cost attacks<\/h2>\n<p>A public <strong>API<\/strong> (<em>Application Programming\nInterface<\/em>) provides programmatic entry points through which\nautomated clients can also submit requests. The greatest danger is not\nmerely overloading a server. With an AI API, even a small number of\nexpensive jobs can incur substantial external costs.<\/p>\n<p>The OWASP <strong>API Security Top 10 from 2023<\/strong> identifies,\namong other things, failures in object authorisation and authentication,\nunauthorised function calls, resource consumption, server-side request\nforgery and unsafe processing of external API responses. The year is\nimportant: this is a specific edition of a maintained security overview,\nnot a claim that 2023 will remain the only authoritative version in the\nfuture.<\/p>\n<p>Typical countermeasures include:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk<\/th>\n<th>Basic defensive principle<\/th>\n<th>Limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer retrieves another person’s job<\/td>\n<td>Server-side object authorisation for every request<\/td>\n<td>A login alone is insufficient<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bot generates millions of calls<\/td>\n<td><strong>Rate limit<\/strong>, i.e. limiting calls per identity/time\nperiod<\/td>\n<td>Distributed abuse may use multiple identities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI job incurs enormous computing costs<\/td>\n<td>Size limits, balance/cost budget, quota before job acceptance<\/td>\n<td>Prices and actual consumption may fluctuate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uploaded file contains malicious content<\/td>\n<td>Format and size checks, isolated processing, restricted\npermissions<\/td>\n<td>A scanner does not detect every danger<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API accesses an address supplied by the user<\/td>\n<td>Restrict destinations and control server-side network requests<\/td>\n<td>Openly configured redirects remain dangerous<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spam or mass account creation<\/td>\n<td>Graduated abuse checks, suitable quotas<\/td>\n<td>Excessively strict barriers burden legitimate users<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In <strong>Server-Side Request Forgery<\/strong> (SSRF), a server is\ninduced to access unintended network destinations. This is particularly\nrelevant if a service loads arbitrary URLs or sends webhooks to freely\nentered destinations. AI systems introduce additional risks, such as\nprompt injection: untrusted text tries to influence the behaviour of an\nAI workflow. Actual access rights must therefore not be enforced solely\nthrough verbal instructions to a model.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>cost attack<\/strong> in this context means that someone\nuses a function in such a way that the operator incurs high bills or\nexperiences resource shortages. A general rate limit is often\ninsufficient if individual calls have very different computing costs.\nLimits must also address the actual resource — for example upload size,\ncomputing time, token consumption or the number of jobs permitted to run\nconcurrently.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Unknown client\n       │\n       ▼\n  Rate limit / input checks\n       │\n       ▼\n  Login + object authorisation\n       │\n       ▼\n  Size, cost and job budget\n       │\n       ▼\n   Job in queue ─► Restricted worker ─► Result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A blanket recommendation that ‘more cloud = more security’ would be\nmistaken. More components also mean more security configuration, keys,\ninterfaces and monitoring.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data protection: When are data personal data?<\/h2>\n<p>The European <strong>General Data Protection Regulation<\/strong>\n(GDPR; German: DSGVO) regulates, among other things, the processing of\npersonal data. Personal data are information relating to an identified\nor identifiable natural person. Typical examples may include a name,\nemail address, customer identifier, IP address or certain usage data if\nthey can be associated with a person.<\/p>\n<p>The context matters. An apparently harmless internal number may be\npersonal data if the service uses it to identify a customer account.\nConversely, genuinely anonymised data must generally be assessed\ndifferently from merely <strong>pseudonymised<\/strong> data. A pseudonym\nmay, for example, replace a name with an identifier, but does not\ncompletely rule out possible re-identification.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data protection is therefore not merely a question of\ndatabase encryption.<\/strong> It begins with deciding whether and why\ndata are collected. For a free web calculator without user accounts, for\nexample, it may be unnecessary to create complete user profiles. A\npersonal history function, by contrast, may need data — but preferably\nonly the data required for that purpose.<\/p>\n<p>The following principles help with design: purpose limitation, data\nminimisation, transparency, storage limitation,\nintegrity/confidentiality and accountability. Processing requires an\nappropriate <strong>legal basis<\/strong>; a blanket statement that\n‘everything always requires consent’ would be inaccurate. Which legal\nbasis is suitable in each individual case must be assessed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Controller and processor: Who decides and who carries out\nthe processing?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>controller<\/strong> within the meaning of the GDPR decides\non the purposes and essential means of processing. A\n<strong>processor<\/strong> processes personal data on behalf of the\ncontroller and in accordance with the controller’s documented\ninstructions, insofar as the respective contractual and legal\nrelationship actually provides for this.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a small online tool: the operator determines which customer\ndata are needed to process jobs and how long they are stored. If the\noperator uses a hosting provider, that provider may act as a processor\nin relation to certain hosting activities. Under the relevant\nconditions, a data processing agreement (DPA; in EU law a contract under\nArticle 28 GDPR) is required. <strong>Not every cloud provider involved\nis automatically merely a processor in every respect<\/strong>; the roles\ndepend on the particular activity.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer ─► Online service operator\n                │ decides purposes and means\n                ▼\n              Controller\n                │ commissions processing\n                ▼\n            Hosting/cloud provider\n              (possibly a processor)\n                │\n                └── Possibly further sub-processors<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Particularly with AI services, an external model provider may also be\ninvolved. It is then necessary to establish whether uploaded texts or\nfiles are transmitted there, whether the provider uses them for its own\npurposes, how long they are retained and which contractual rules apply.\nWithout this information, the data protection role cannot be assessed\nreliably.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Practical consequence:<\/strong> A GDPR obligation does not\ndisappear merely because another provider operates the computers. At the\nsame time, the operator cannot control everything that happens in\nexternal data centres; contracts, technical settings and verifiable\ndocumentation therefore matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Storage location, international transfers and erasure<\/h2>\n<p>The physical location of a database matters but is not the only data\nprotection issue. Data may reach other parties during backups, support\naccess, through subcontractors or via an external AI API. ‘EU server’\ntherefore does not automatically mean that all processing takes place\nexclusively within the European Union.<\/p>\n<p>Special requirements apply to certain transfers of personal data\noutside the <strong>European Economic Area<\/strong> (EEA). Legal\ninstruments may include adequacy decisions by the European Commission or\nappropriate safeguards including <strong>standard contractual\nclauses<\/strong>. Which option is sufficient in each case depends on the\ndestination country, the parties involved and further circumstances.\nLists and legal situations may change; decisions should be assessed\nagainst the current position.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Erasure<\/strong> sounds simple but becomes complicated in\nreal online services: data may reside in the primary database, a search\nindex, a worker, temporary files, log data and backups. A traceable\nerasure concept must take these storage locations into account and —\nwhere required — incorporate statutory retention obligations and the\ntechnically and legally appropriate treatment of existing backups.\nMerely removing data from the user interface is insufficient.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer file\n   ├── Active database\n   ├── Temporary processing\n   ├── Search index or result cache\n   ├── Logs (ideally without file contents)\n   └── Backup copies with their own retention period<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Privacy by design<\/strong> and <strong>privacy by\ndefault<\/strong> mean providing for data-minimising architecture and\nsettings from the outset. This includes, for example, retaining an\nuploaded job after completion only for as long as necessary for the\nspecified purpose and legally permitted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Information obligations, rights and security incidents<\/h2>\n<p>Anyone processing personal data must, under the applicable legal\nconditions, inform the data subjects who processes the data, for what\npurposes, on what legal basis and for what retention period. Data\nsubject rights may also become relevant, particularly access,\nrectification or erasure; each right is subject to conditions and\npossible exceptions.<\/p>\n<p>If personal information is accidentally disclosed, altered, destroyed\nor rendered inaccessible, a <strong>personal data breach<\/strong> may\nhave occurred. Not every technical failure is automatically a reportable\npersonal data breach. Under the GDPR, a controller must generally notify\nthe competent supervisory authority of a relevant personal data breach\nwithout undue delay and, where feasible, within <strong>72 hours of\nbecoming aware of it<\/strong>, <strong>unless the breach is unlikely to\nresult in a risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons<\/strong>.\nWhere a high risk is likely, notifying the affected individuals may\nadditionally be required. Personal data breaches that are not reported\nmust also be documented under Article 33(5). This must be assessed in\nlight of the particular incident.<\/p>\n<p>This sounds formal but has a direct technical prerequisite: if no one\ncan determine which data were affected, it is also difficult to assess\nan incident appropriately. Limited logging, defined responsibilities and\na simple emergency procedure are therefore important even for small\nservices.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Economic consequence:<\/strong> Data protection may entail\ncosts for design, documentation, contract review, support for access and\nerasure requests, and secure handling of failures. A software idea of\nlittle benefit that would require processing particularly sensitive data\nshould therefore be critically assessed for its economic and\norganisational scope.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three applications: From a local tool to an AI service<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-a-local-media-archive\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A: A local media\narchive<\/h3>\n<p>A program catalogues files and metadata on your own computer. Without\npublic accessibility or other users, the risk is typically more\nmanageable. System updates, reliable local permissions and separate\nbackups remain worthwhile. If images or information about identifiable\npeople are stored or published, additional data protection and\npersonality rights issues may arise.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is a large security solution unnecessary?<\/strong> If\nonly your own user account actually accesses purely local data, a\ncomplete cloud IAM service is usually unnecessary. Simplicity is then\nitself a security advantage.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-a-small-paid-online-analysis-tool\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B: A small\npaid online analysis tool<\/h3>\n<p>A customer registers, uploads a file and receives an analysis. The\nservice now needs secure login, careful object assignment, limited file\nsizes, protection against other people’s files, an erasure rule and\npossibly a DPA with the hosting provider. If payment data are processed,\nit must be assessed whether a specialist payment service can take over\nthe storage of payment information requiring particular protection.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer ─► Login ─► File upload ─► Job check\n                                       │\n                               Own customer area\n                                       │\n                               Isolated processing\n                                       │\n                               Result + erasure rule<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A small conventional VPS or a managed service may suffice. Costs\narise not only from the server but also from security updates, user\nenquiries and the risk of faulty upload processing.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"example-c-an-ai-service-with-multiple-workers-and-external-models\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example\nC: An AI service with multiple workers and external models<\/h3>\n<p>In AI workflows, the number of trust boundaries increases. A job\nserver receives data, a scheduler distributes work, and workers use GPU\nresources (<em>Graphics Processing Unit<\/em>, graphics processor) or\nsend data to external models. Some jobs may be expensive; some input\ndata may contain personal information.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer\n  │\n  ▼\nAPI + quota + customer permissions\n  │\n  ▼\nJob database ─► Queue\n                  │\n            ┌─────┴───────────────────┐\n            ▼                         ▼\n      Local AI worker          External model provider\n            │                         │\n            └─────────────┬───────────┘\n                          ▼\n                Result only for the customer\n                          │\n                  Storage/erasure rules<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Limits on model calls, specific machine access credentials,\ndocumentation of data flows and careful assessment of external providers\nbecome additionally important here. Texts, URLs or suggested commands\ngenerated by the model must not be executed without control as\nadministrative instructions. For a small initial version, it may be more\neconomical to offer <strong>just one worker and a tightly restricted\nAPI<\/strong> instead of immediately combining complex agent systems and\nmultiple cloud services.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The central economic question: Which risks can I manage in\nthe long term?<\/h2>\n<p>A secure system is not simply the one with the most products and\ncertificates. Security measures must suit actual operations. An\nindividual developer can often understand and maintain a simple\napplication better than an architecture of many independently updated\nservices.<\/p>\n<p>For every product idea of your own, at least five questions should be\nanswered: What data are processed? Who may access what? Which external\nproviders receive data or keys? How are misuse and unexpected costs\nlimited? How are data backed up, erased and handled during security\nincidents?<\/p>\n<p>Recurring operating costs arise from updates, security checks, log\nstorage, access controls, backup, possible external specialist advice\nand support. Managed services can reduce workloads, but they cannot\nsimply remove responsibility for designing your own application and\nselecting its settings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M4-L4<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Security<\/strong> protects systems and information against\nunauthorised interference, alterations and outages. <strong>Data\nprotection<\/strong> additionally requires that the processing of\npersonal data be justified and restricted legally and organisationally.\nAuthentication, authorisation, secrets management, encryption, abuse\nprotection and backup solve different problems; none of these measures\nreplaces the others.<\/p>\n<p>For a small online service, a few consistently implemented principles\nare often more important than a large security platform: as little\nstored data as possible, server-side authorisation checks, separate\nsecrets, limited resources, traceable updates and tested recovery. With\nincreasing user numbers and external integrations, the requirements for\noperations and documentation increase.<\/p>\n<p><strong>In M4-L4 – Understanding costs<\/strong>, we consider the\neconomic implications of these and other technical decisions: fixed and\nvariable cloud costs, data traffic, GPU time, budgets and the choice\nbetween a small server, a platform and a complex cluster.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>All the following primary or project sources were checked on\n<strong>03.11.2026<\/strong> for accessibility and relevance. They\nprovide guidance for this lesson; a further check for currency is\nnecessary for specific production or legal use.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>NIST, <em>SP 800-63B-4 – Digital Identity Guidelines: Authentication\nand Authenticator Management<\/em> (2025 edition): <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/63/b/4/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/63/b/4/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OWASP, <em>API Security Top 10 – 2023<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://api-security.owasp.org/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/\">https://api-security.owasp.org/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OWASP, <em>Password Storage Cheat Sheet<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OWASP, <em>Secrets Management Cheat Sheet<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OWASP, <em>Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Commission, <em>Data protection explained<\/em> (roles and\npersonal data): <a\nhref=\"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/data-protection-explained_en\">https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/data-protection-explained_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Commission, <em>Obligations<\/em> (information obligations):\n<a\nhref=\"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/obligations_en\">https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/obligations_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Data Protection Board, <em>Secure personal data<\/em> /\nguide for small businesses: <a\nhref=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/be-compliant/secure-personal-data_de\">https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/be-compliant/secure-personal-data_de<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Data Protection Board, <em>Data breaches<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/assess-the-risks/data-breaches_en\">https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/assess-the-risks/data-breaches_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Data Protection Board, <em>Be compliant<\/em> (Privacy by\nDesign): <a\nhref=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/be-compliant/be-compliant_en\">https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/be-compliant/be-compliant_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Commission, <em>Rules on international data transfers<\/em>:\n<a\nhref=\"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/rules-international-data-transfers_en\">https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/rules-international-data-transfers_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Commission, <em>Adequacy decisions<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/adequacy-decisions_en\">https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/adequacy-decisions_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M4-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M4-L4 – Understanding costs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor’s / transition: Cloud operations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: A functioning architecture is not\nnecessarily an economical architecture<\/h2>\n<p>A self-developed online tool can work extremely well technically and\nstill be uneconomical. A small application might need only one server.\nIf you additionally operate a managed database cluster, a load balancer,\nseveral container nodes, an external logging service and an elaborate\nnetwork, users do not automatically pay more for the function — but\nrunning costs increase.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, a very inexpensive solution can become costly if it\nconstantly fails and creates a great deal of manual work. When operating\nyour own online service, the question should therefore be not only\n<strong>‘How much does the server cost?’<\/strong> but also: <strong>‘How\nmuch does the complete, reliably usable function cost per month and per\nactual job?’<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A <em>cost model<\/em> here means a simplified, traceable calculation\nconnecting the resources used with their billing and business value. It\nis not a description of a specific provider. Particularly with cloud\nservices, costs can arise even when nobody is visiting the website: for\nreserved capacity, stored data, backups, IP addresses or network\ncomponents.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Users / jobs\n       │\n       ▼\nApplication + database + files\n       │\n       ├── Computing time and memory\n       ├── Storage space and backups\n       ├── Networks and data transfer\n       ├── External APIs / AI models\n       └── Monitoring, maintenance, support\n                        │\n                        ▼\n             Total cost of the service\n                        │\n                        ▼\n                Cost per service delivered<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem does cost accounting solve?<\/strong> It makes\nexpenditure visible, enables fair comparisons and exposes risky\nassumptions. <strong>When is elaborate financial management\nunnecessary?<\/strong> For a single private server, a simple spreadsheet\nor list of monthly cost items is often sufficient initially. An\nelaborate financial management system would create additional work\nwithout corresponding insight.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two fundamental types of cost: Fixed and\nusage-dependent<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Fixed costs<\/strong> arise within a particular period under\nconsideration even when there is little or no usage. Typical examples\ninclude a virtual machine rented monthly, a fixed web hosting plan, a\ndomain, a paid monitoring package or depreciation of purchased hardware.\n‘Fixed’ does not mean unchangeable forever: a tariff or contract may be\nadjusted later.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Variable costs<\/strong> increase with measured usage: for\nexample per executed function, gigabyte-month of storage, volume of data\nprocessed, computing hour or model call. A service may combine both\nmodels. For instance, a managed database service may charge a base\namount for the continuously running instance plus additional charges for\nstorage or backup space.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Marginal costs<\/strong> are the additional costs of one more\njob or customer. If spare capacity is available on an already paid-for\nserver, the immediate additional cost of a small job may be very low.\nHowever, if the next increase in load requires a second machine, costs\nrise abruptly. A tariff with fixed tiers does not behave like a fully\nlinear pricing model either.<\/p>\n<p>A useful approximation is:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Monthly costs = recurring base costs\n              + usage charges\n              + other operating and support costs\n\nCost per completed job = attributable total costs\n                       / successfully completed jobs<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Failed attempts, retries and cancelled jobs belong in the total\ncosts. For a service that attempts 10,000 jobs but successfully\ncompletes only 8,000, the cost of a usable result should not be\ncalculated by dividing by 10,000. This is precisely why a simple ‘cost\nper API call’ metric is often insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What cloud providers actually charge for<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"31-prozessorzeit-instanzlaufzeit-und-arbeitsspeicher\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Processor\ntime, instance runtime and memory<\/h3>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000089_en.png\"\nalt=\"Cloud cost categories without fictitious percentages\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Cloud cost categories without fictitious\npercentages<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A <strong>CPU<\/strong> (<em>Central Processing Unit<\/em>, main\nprocessor) performs general computations. Some providers charge for the\nreserved computing capacity of a machine according to its runtime,\nregardless of whether it operates permanently at 100 per cent. Other\nservices bill actual runtime or invoked functions according to their own\npricing model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>RAM<\/strong> (<em>Random Access Memory<\/em>, working memory)\nis often booked together with a machine class. For some serverless\nservices, the selected memory influences the billed unit of computing.\nAdditional RAM may be necessary for a memory-intensive application, but\ncannot automatically repair a slow database design.<\/p>\n<p>A rented virtual machine may, for example, be billed continuously for\nas long as it exists or runs — the exact rule depends on the tariff. A\nshort-lived function, by contrast, is often billed only for a measurable\nexecution, plus other components. ‘Pay for usage’ does not mean that the\nwhole system is necessarily free when idle.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"32-gpu-zeit-und-ki-verarbeitung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>GPU time and AI processing<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>GPU<\/strong> (<em>Graphics Processing Unit<\/em>, originally\na graphics processor) can perform large parallel computations and is\nparticularly important for numerous AI models. It often incurs\nsignificantly higher hourly rates than a small CPU instance. What\nmatters, therefore, is not only the hourly price but <strong>how long an\nexpensive GPU is actually occupied<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>An example using a purely hypothetical hourly rate of 8 euros:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">3 hours per day, 30 days:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>720<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">24 hours per day, 30 days:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>24<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>760<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{3 hours per day, 30 days:}\\quad&amp;3\\times30\\times8\\,\\text{€}=720\\,\\text{€}\\\\\n\\text{24 hours per day, 30 days:}\\quad&amp;24\\times30\\times8\\,\\text{€}=5\\,760\\,\\text{€}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The calculation illustrates the difference between temporary and\ncontinuous provision. It does <em>not<\/em> claim that GPU offerings\ncurrently cost eight euros, and does not yet account for CPU, RAM,\nstorage, network, data or licence costs. Even a switched-off GPU\ninstance may continue to incur costs for disks, allocated addresses or\nstorage, depending on the offering.<\/p>\n<p>For <strong>inference<\/strong> — running an already trained model —\nyou can compute locally, rent a dedicated GPU or use an external\n<strong>API<\/strong> (<em>Application Programming Interface<\/em>).\nExternal text-model APIs may, for example, charge for input and output\ntokens. A <em>token<\/em> is a processing unit of a language model; it is\nnot always identical to a whole word. Other AI services charge by\nimages, seconds, computing time or other units. <strong>The unit must be\nidentified from the specific offering before each\ncalculation.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"33-datentraeger-datenbank-und-backups\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Disks, databases and\nbackups<\/h3>\n<p>Stored data may be billed according to capacity and duration. A\ntypical unit is the <strong>GB-month<\/strong>: one gigabyte occupied for\napproximately one month. However, not every bill uses the same\ndefinition of a gigabyte; tariffs may distinguish decimal GB from binary\nGiB.<\/p>\n<p>Besides storage space, other factors often count:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Number of read and write operations and provisioned input/output\nperformance.<\/li>\n<li>Type of storage, such as fast block storage or less expensive object\nstorage.<\/li>\n<li>Database storage, transaction logs and managed database\noperation.<\/li>\n<li>Snapshots, full backups, versioning and retention.<\/li>\n<li>Retrieval and recovery charges from certain storage classes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>A backup is not a free by-product of a database.<\/strong> If\nnew backups are created daily, data volume and retention duration may\nbecome relevant. An apparently inexpensive storage class is not\nnecessarily the most economical for frequent retrieval or long minimum\nretention periods. Nevertheless, the security of data is not assessed\nsolely on the basis of the storage price (see M3-L6).<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"34-datenverkehr-und-egress\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data traffic and egress<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Egress<\/strong> refers to outgoing data traffic from a\ndefined network or service. In particular, transmitting cloud data to\nusers on the public internet may incur costs depending on the tariff.\nSome providers distinguish between incoming and outgoing data, between\nregions and availability zones, and between internal and external\ntransfers.<\/p>\n<p>For example, an online service delivers many images of several\nmegabytes each every month. Its CPU costs are low, but the total volume\nof transferred files may be high. A <strong>CDN<\/strong> (<em>Content\nDelivery Network<\/em>, distributed delivery network) can cache data\ncloser to users, but a CDN also has its own cost model — it does not\nautomatically make file transfers free.<\/p>\n<p>In distributed architectures, additional east-west traffic occurs:\ndatabase replication, synchronisation between zones and data movement\nbetween services. Related services should therefore be located sensibly\nwherever possible; unnecessary large data movements may be more\nexpensive than the actual processing.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"35-kleinere-posten-die-sich-summieren\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Smaller items that add\nup<\/h3>\n<p>Additional costs may arise for domains and certificate services, DNS\nrequests (<em>Domain Name System<\/em>, name resolution), load balancers,\n<strong>NAT gateways<\/strong> (<em>Network Address Translation<\/em>,\nmediated outbound network access), IP addresses, logs, metrics,\nnotifications, secrets management or special support plans.<\/p>\n<p>One example from vendor documentation: AWS explicitly notes that NAT\ngateways can incur both time-based and data-based fees and that unused\ngateways represent an opportunity to save costs. This does not mean that\nevery operator needs a NAT gateway. Rather, it illustrates why the\narchitecture should be simplified <strong>before<\/strong> unnecessary\ncomponents are set up.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How deployment models change the bill<\/h2>\n<p>The following models were explained technically in M2-L3 and M2-L4.\nHere we consider the same models economically. The table deliberately\ncontains <strong>no current market prices<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th>Typical billing<\/th>\n<th>What is easily overlooked<\/th>\n<th>Suitable when …<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conventional web hosting provider<\/td>\n<td>Monthly tariff<\/td>\n<td>Execution limits, database limits, performance limits<\/td>\n<td>A PHP site or small web service can remain simple<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VPS<\/strong> (<em>Virtual Private Server<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Usually rented machine capacity<\/td>\n<td>Updates, backup, outages, administration<\/td>\n<td>A manageable application runs continuously<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PaaS<\/strong> (<em>Platform as a Service<\/em>, managed\napplication platform)<\/td>\n<td>Instance, usage, additional services<\/td>\n<td>Database, storage, transfer, limits<\/td>\n<td>Less server operation of your own is desired<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Serverless<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Calls, execution time and other consumption measures<\/td>\n<td>Ancillary services, minimum runtimes, peak load, start-up delay<\/td>\n<td>Irregular, short, independent tasks arise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Container platform<\/td>\n<td>Nodes/containers plus infrastructure<\/td>\n<td>Base cluster costs, network, observability<\/td>\n<td>Several services must be managed uniformly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kubernetes cluster<\/td>\n<td>Machines, control plane depending on offering, network, storage<\/td>\n<td>Operational effort and idle capacity<\/td>\n<td>Scale and requirements justify complexity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Own hardware<\/td>\n<td>Purchase plus ongoing operation<\/td>\n<td>Electricity, ageing, replacement, location, accessibility<\/td>\n<td>Local control or consistent utilisation matters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>VPS<\/strong> is not outdated technology. For a small PHP\napplication or a compact API, it can often provide the best balance of\ncost and control. A <strong>PaaS<\/strong> may appear more expensive but\nreduces certain administrative activities. A serverless model may be\neconomical for irregular usage, whereas a service that is constantly\nheavily loaded may run more cheaply with reserved capacity. There are\nexceptions in both directions.<\/p>\n<p>Kubernetes can standardise the management of many application\ninstances. <strong>But it does not solve an economic problem merely by\nexisting.<\/strong> In particular, a small one-person service may acquire\nunnecessary complexity, background consumption and troubleshooting\nthrough cluster technology.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three calculated models — explicitly not provider\noffers<\/h2>\n<p>The following <strong>fictitious model prices<\/strong> illustrate\nonly the calculation method. They are not suitable as a direct basis for\na purchase decision; a real calculation must determine prices, free\nallowances, regions, taxes, runtimes and actual loads afresh.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-small-continuously-accessible-online-tool\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A:\nSmall, continuously accessible online tool<\/h3>\n<p>Suppose a PHP application needs a small virtual server and a local\ndatabase. We arbitrarily assume:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Fictitious monthly costs<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>VPS<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External backup retention<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain, proportion per month<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small reserve for additional costs<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>21 €<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>With 700 successfully completed analyses per month, this corresponds\nto\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>21<\/mn><mn>700<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>03<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Euro<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{21}{700}=0{,}03\\,\\text{Euro}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nof direct technical costs per result. The calculation excludes working\ntime for maintenance, security updates, customer service, development\nand business administration. It also omits a second machine for fault\ntolerance.<\/p>\n<p>If the application runs reliably on a single server, there is no\neconomic reason to turn it immediately into six independent services. If\nhigher load is foreseeable, the first step should be to investigate\nwhether indexes, caching and resource-efficient software can produce\nimprovements.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-the-same-function-on-a-managed-platform\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B:\nThe same function on a managed platform<\/h3>\n<p>Fictitious monthly assumptions:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Fictitious monthly costs<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Application platform<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed database<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>File storage and backups<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data traffic and logs<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4 €<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>38 €<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In this model calculation, the platform costs 17 euros more than the\nsingle VPS. It does <strong>not<\/strong> follow that it is 17 euros\nworse. It may save staff time for operations, updates and manual\nrecovery steps; it may also have additional limitations. A fair\ncomparison must consider the same scope of security, availability and\nbackups.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"example-c-infrequent-computing-jobs-as-a-serverless-function\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example\nC: Infrequent computing jobs as a serverless function<\/h3>\n<p>Suppose a platform were to charge a purely hypothetical 0.40 euros\nper million calls and 0.000020 euros per billed\n<strong>GB-second<\/strong>. The GB-second unit combines allocated memory\nand execution time. In this model, one job needs 0.5 GB for 0.2 seconds;\nthere are one million jobs per month.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Memory time:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">GB-seconds<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Computing component:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>000020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Call component:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Total model function costs:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Memory time:}\\quad&amp;1\\,000\\,000\\times0{,}5\\,\\mathrm{GB}\\times0{,}2\\,\\mathrm{s}\n   =100\\,000\\,\\text{GB-seconds}\\\\\n\\text{Computing component:}\\quad&amp;100\\,000\\times0{,}000020\\,\\text{€}=2{,}00\\,\\text{€}\\\\\n\\text{Call component:}\\quad&amp;\\frac{1\\,000\\,000}{1\\,000\\,000}\\times0{,}40\\,\\text{€}=0{,}40\\,\\text{€}\\\\\n\\text{Total model function costs:}\\quad&amp;2{,}40\\,\\text{€}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>These <strong>2.40 euros are not the complete system costs<\/strong>.\nThe function may need a database, network traffic, authentication, logs\nand a persistent data store. Some platforms have minimum billing units,\nfixed fees, free allowances or memory and runtime limits. And a function\nthat runs for ten seconds instead of 0.2 seconds generates 50 times the\ncomputing costs at the same fictitious linear rate. The cost profile may\ntherefore change fundamentally under continuously high load.<\/p>\n<p>The example shows both the advantage and the trap of\nconsumption-based billing: costs may be low when idle; without limits, a\nload spike can increase the bill.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Own hardware: Costs are more than the electricity bill<\/h2>\n<p>A home computer, mini-PC or your own AI computer can make\ncost-effective use of existing computing power — particularly for\nprivate processing without permanent public accessibility. However,\neconomic comparisons also include <strong>purchase, depreciation,\nelectricity, wear and operational risks<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Fictitious example: a computer consumes an average of 90 watts, or\n0.09 kilowatts, during the continuous operation under consideration.\nWith 30 days and an explicitly assumed electricity price of 0.30 euros\nper kilowatt-hour, this gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>09<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">hours<\/mtext><mo>×<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">days<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>64<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€/kWh<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>44<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">€ electricity/month<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n0{,}09\\,\\mathrm{kW}\\times24\\,\\text{hours}\\times30\\,\\text{days}&amp;=64{,}8\\,\\mathrm{kWh}\\\\\n64{,}8\\,\\mathrm{kWh}\\times0{,}30\\,\\text{€/kWh}&amp;=19{,}44\\,\\text{€ electricity/month}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the device hypothetically cost 600 euros, a simple economic\noverview could include\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Euro<\/mtext><\/mrow><mrow><mn>36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">months<\/mtext><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>67<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Euro per month<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{600\\,\\text{Euro}}{36\\,\\text{months}}\\approx16{,}67\\,\\text{Euro per month}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTogether, this would already amount to approximately 36.11 euros per\nmonth, <strong>excluding<\/strong> networking, replacement parts,\nbackups, failure precautions, maintenance and working time. An\naccounting allocation of purchase costs is not an actual monthly cash\noutflow; it merely makes long-term total costs comparable.<\/p>\n<p>If you already own the computer and would have switched it on anyway,\nthe <strong>additional<\/strong> cost of individual local tasks may be\nsignificantly smaller. If, by contrast, a commercial online service is\nto be permanently accessible through it, further questions arise:\ninternet connection, public accessibility, power failures, noise and\nheat, data protection and separation of private and publicly offered\nsystems. A home computer does not automatically replace the\ninfrastructure of a data centre.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is self-hosting sensible?<\/strong> When control,\nexisting hardware, data protection requirements or a well-utilised GPU\njustify the effort. <strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> When a\nservice is called only occasionally and reliable public accessibility is\nmore important than saving individual usage fees.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI costs: Three fundamentally different approaches<\/h2>\n<p>For AI applications, at least three approaches should be\ndistinguished:<\/p>\n<p><strong>External model API:<\/strong> A provider processes jobs.\nDepending on the model, charges may be per token, image, minute or\nexecution time. Advantage: no GPU of your own and less model operation.\nDisadvantage: provider dependency, prices and limits may change; data\nprotection, latency and maximum usage must be assessed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rented GPU for your own model inference:<\/strong> Your own\nmodel runs on a GPU instance. Advantage: more control over the model,\ndata flow and computation method. Disadvantage: costs for runtime, model\nstorage, start-up processes and operations, even when the machine is not\nfully utilised.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Local GPU:<\/strong> Inference takes place on existing\nhardware. Advantage: no usage-dependent model provider bill and, with\nlocal operation, usually no need to send inputs to an external model\nAPI. Disadvantage: energy demand, limited graphics memory, hardware\nwear, maintenance and possibly insufficient availability for external\ncustomers.<\/p>\n<p>The decisive metric is not simply ‘price per GPU-hour’ but, for\nexample, <strong>cost per finished, usable image<\/strong> or\n<strong>cost per successfully completed video job<\/strong>. With a slow\nmodel, low hourly rates may be offset by long processing times.\nConversely, a more expensive, faster system may cost less per result at\nhigh utilisation.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>AI job\n   │\n   ├── External API: billing according to service model\n   │\n   ├── Rented GPU: runtime + data + operations\n   │\n   └── Local GPU: electricity + hardware + maintenance\n   │\n   ▼\nSuccessful result\n   │\n   ▼\nCost per usable result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An AI pipeline may also require multiple model calls: create an\nimage, evaluate it, improve it, derive a video, store it. Discarded\nintermediate results also cost computing time and potentially API fees.\n<strong>A business model must factor in the expected proportion of\nrejected results.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cost traps and unexpected bills<\/h2>\n<p>Not every increase in costs means more paying users. Typical causes\ninclude:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Unused resources running continuously:<\/strong> forgotten\ntest instances, databases or cluster nodes.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Automatic scaling without a practical upper limit:<\/strong>\nmore instances during abusive or erroneous load.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Retry loops:<\/strong> workers or agents trigger infinitely\nmany new tasks because of an error.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Underestimated data traffic:<\/strong> large images, video\nfiles, backups or transfers between regions.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Unlimited log and backup retention:<\/strong> small\nquantities become large volumes of stored data in the long term.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chargeable ancillary services:<\/strong> load balancers, NAT\ngateways, monitoring, network components or managed databases.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Misunderstood free allowances:<\/strong> after the free quota\nis exceeded, usage may become chargeable; the exact behaviour is\nprovider-specific.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Resources not fully terminated:<\/strong> stopping a machine\ndoes not automatically delete all disks, snapshots or addresses.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Application errors and abuse:<\/strong> unauthorised API\nusage or other people’s jobs may incur charges despite low legitimate\ndemand.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Cost control is therefore also a matter of <strong>software\narchitecture and security<\/strong> (see M4-L1 and M4-L3), not just\naccounting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Budgets, alerts, quotas and hard limits<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>budget<\/strong> may initially be a planned maximum amount.\nCloud providers usually offer billing views and notifications for this\npurpose. A <strong>cost alert<\/strong> reports that a threshold has been\nreached or is expected to be reached. It is <strong>not inherently an\nautomatic spending block<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>This distinction matters and can be clarified using official\nsources:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microsoft explains that Azure Cost Management budgets trigger\nnotifications but <strong>do not stop resources\nautomatically<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>AWS notes that billing data and budget notifications may arrive with\ndelays; further costs can arise even after a warning. Specific separate\nbudget actions or spend-limit functions must be assessed for each\nprovider.<\/li>\n<li>Google Cloud distinguishes between alert-only budgets and\n<strong>spend cap budgets<\/strong> available for certain services. An\nalert-only budget does not limit the bill. Even actual spend caps may\ninterrupt the service and have their own scope. <strong>As of\n05.11.2026:<\/strong> Google describes such limits for one supported\nservice at a time within a project, including Gemini API, Agent\nPlatform, Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions. When the limit is reached,\nthe platform pauses new usage of the affected service; requests already\nin progress may continue to incur costs. Other services, persistent\nresources and fixed costs may continue. Enforcement is not instantaneous\nand overspending may be billed. A spend cap is therefore <strong>not an\noverall price ceiling for the entire cloud project<\/strong>. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets-spend-caps\">Google\nCloud – Spend Cap Budgets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>There are consequently different levels of protection:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Cost forecast / budget          → Detect early\nNotifications                   → Inform the operator\nTechnical quotas / rate limits  → Limit usage\nJob and customer quotas         → Limit individual sources of costs\nAutomatic shutdown logic        → If actually available and tested\nBill review                     → Trace the results<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Examples of technical limits include a maximum permitted number of\nconcurrent GPU jobs, a limited file size, a monthly API quota per user,\na fixed upper limit for workers and termination after too many retries.\nSuch measures can prevent a programming error from generating computing\njobs without limit. <strong>They nevertheless do not guarantee an\nabsolutely limited bill<\/strong> if other cost items or delays are not\ntaken into account.<\/p>\n<p>For a small service, a manageable control routine is often\nsufficient: identify cost centres, set warning thresholds below the\ndesired maximum amount, compare actual bills and incorporate technical\nlimits in the application itself. Additional payment obligations or tax\nissues must be assessed separately for a real offering.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical cost planning in three stages<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"stage-1-minimum-monthly-requirements\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stage 1: Minimum monthly\nrequirements<\/h3>\n<p>Initially, one question is enough: which <strong>services actually\nneed to run all the time<\/strong>? For example, a web application, a\ndatabase and a backup. All other components are initially treated as\noptional. This produces a clear list of base costs.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"stage-2-three-load-scenarios\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stage 2: Three load scenarios<\/h3>\n<p>Instead of using a single optimistic figure, three scenarios are\nuseful:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low usage:<\/strong> only a few jobs, but base costs\ncontinue.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Expected usage:<\/strong> average number of successful jobs\nwith normal data volume.<\/li>\n<li><strong>High or unusual usage:<\/strong> many simultaneous jobs,\nlarger files, additional errors and retry attempts.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A price comparison is meaningful only if these scenarios are\n<strong>comparable<\/strong> across the alternatives. A highly available\nKubernetes cluster and a single VPS without external backup do not offer\nthe same service quality.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"stage-3-full-costs-and-value-per-job\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stage 3: Full costs and\nvalue per job<\/h3>\n<p>For a commercial application of our own, we add the actual or\ncalculated costs of maintenance, development, support, software\nlicences, payment processing and taxes where applicable to the directly\nbilled cloud costs. This yields <strong>full costs<\/strong>, or a\n<strong>Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) assessment<\/strong>: not just the\npurchase or rental, but the economic burden over the period under\nconsideration.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>FinOps Foundation<\/strong> (<em>Financial\nOperations<\/em>, collaborative management of technology expenditure and\nits value) recommends relating expenditure to meaningful units of\nservice. This is called <em>Unit Economics<\/em>. For an analysis tool of\nour own, this might be ‘cost per successful analysis’. For AI images, it\nwould be ‘cost per usable result’, not merely ‘cost per token’ or ‘cost\nper minute of server time’.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Technical resources\n        │\n        ▼\nDirect operating costs\n        │\n        + Maintenance / support / replacement / other obligations\n        │\n        ▼\nTotal costs over a period\n        │\n        ▼\nAllocate to successfully delivered services\n        │\n        ▼\nCost per analysis, file, customer or AI result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem does this metric solve?<\/strong> It helps\nidentify whether growing revenue is being consumed by technical costs\nthat grow even faster. <strong>When is it unnecessarily\nelaborate?<\/strong> For a purely private, non-monetised script,\nmonitoring electricity, storage and any monthly hosting fee is usually\nsufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which solution makes economic sense in which\nsituation?<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-1-documentation-website-and-smaller-php-tools\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example\n1: Documentation website and smaller PHP tools<\/h3>\n<p>For a relatively static website with a few dynamic functions,\nconventional web hosting or a small VPS is usually sufficient. The\noperator needs a domain, web server, secure configuration, a database if\nnecessary, backups and updates. A Kubernetes cluster or GPU server of\none’s own would probably create no added value. What matters is which\nfunctions are actually needed, not which cloud terms sound particularly\nmodern.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"beispiel-2-online-datenanalyse-mit-unregelmaessigen-auftraegen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example\n2: Online data analysis with irregular jobs<\/h3>\n<p>A small web application running continuously can accept requests and\nplace analysis jobs in a queue. When demand is low, a single worker\nmight suffice; additional workers may become economical during load\nspikes. Alternatively, serverless processing is interesting if the tasks\nare short and the respective provider’s limits are suitable. However,\nthe complete cost chain from invocation, storage and runtime to data\noutput must then be considered.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Small web application → Job database\n                                     │\n                                     ▼\n                                   Queue\n                                     │\n                                     ▼\n                              1 worker, later more\n                                     │\n                                     ▼\n                                 Result file<\/code><\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"example-3-your-own-ai-processing-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example 3: Your own AI\nprocessing service<\/h3>\n<p>Here, an inexpensive web server can trigger expensive purchased GPU\nor API work. Control must therefore address the <strong>actual cost\ndrivers<\/strong>: job type, maximum resolution, permitted runtime,\nutilisation, retries and concurrent jobs. A system that allows\nuncontrolled recomputation may become uneconomical with just a few\nusers.<\/p>\n<p>A sensible initial model is often a small control API with\ntransparently limited processing. Your own local AI hardware can\ninitially be used for experimentation and private batch work. Only when\nreal demand and load profiles are known is a more precise comparison\nwith rented GPUs or external model APIs worthwhile.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Price lists, calculators and vendor lock-in<\/h2>\n<p>Cloud providers make price lists and calculators available. A\n<strong>pricing calculator<\/strong> allows configuration and expected\nusage to be estimated — it does not guarantee the subsequent bill.\nMicrosoft documents, for example, that the Azure Pricing Calculator\ndistinguishes between region, size, service model and usage volume.\nPrices may also differ because of individual contracts, discounts,\nreservations and currencies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Vendor lock-in<\/strong>, or dependency on provider-specific\nfunctionality, is also economically relevant. A proprietary database or\na service available only from that provider may cost time for migration\nand adaptation in the long term. This does not mean that every managed\nservice should be rejected; the reduction in operational work may well\njustify the cost of switching.<\/p>\n<p>Before making a real decision, at least the following points should\ntherefore be checked: current tariff and region, billing unit, set-up or\nminimum costs, free allowance, storage and data traffic, exit and data\nexport costs, security responsibilities, cancellation terms and\navailability of the functions actually needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M4-L5<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Cloud costs rarely consist only of ‘the server’.<\/strong>\nComputing time, memory, storage duration, data transfer, databases,\nbackups, external APIs, monitoring and operational maintenance together\nform the actual economic burden. The decisive distinctions are fixed\nversus variable costs, idle time versus utilisation and direct computing\nprices versus costs per usable result.<\/p>\n<p>Conventional web hosting or a small VPS may suffice for a small\napplication. PaaS reduces certain operational tasks, serverless may suit\nirregular jobs, and cluster platforms help at an appropriate scale — but\nnone of these technologies is inherently the cheapest or best. A budget\nalert is not automatically a hard limit; technical upper limits and bill\nmonitoring are tasks in their own right.<\/p>\n<p><strong>In M4-L5 – Your own software and cloud offerings<\/strong>, we\nconnect this cost knowledge with business models: what could you offer,\nhow can demand be assessed, what minimal first versions are conceivable,\nand when do support, security and ongoing fees exceed the realistic\nbenefit?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Checked on 04.11.2026.<\/strong> The following sources\ndescribe provider functions or cost methods. They are <strong>not a\nprice commitment<\/strong>; this lesson deliberately contains no current\nlist prices.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Microsoft Learn, <em>Estimate costs with the Azure pricing\ncalculator<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/pricing-calculator\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/pricing-calculator<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– influence of region, configuration, usage volume and contracts on cost\nestimates.<\/li>\n<li>Microsoft Learn, <em>Tutorial: Create and manage budgets<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-acm-create-budgets\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-acm-create-budgets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– budget notifications do not stop resources automatically; delayed\nbilling data.<\/li>\n<li>Google Cloud, <em>Create, edit, or delete budgets and budget\nalerts<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– alert-only budgets versus supported hard spending limits.<\/li>\n<li>Google Cloud, <em>Manage spend cap budgets<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets-spend-caps\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets-spend-caps<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– possible spending blocks for supported services and consequences for\naccessibility.<\/li>\n<li>AWS, <em>Managing your costs with AWS Budgets<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/de_de/cost-management/latest/userguide/budgets-managing-costs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/de_de/cost-management/latest/userguide/budgets-managing-costs.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– budget types, alerts and actions.<\/li>\n<li>AWS, <em>Best practices for AWS Budgets<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/budgets-best-practices.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/budgets-best-practices.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– delays and frequency of cost data updates.<\/li>\n<li>AWS, <em>Cost optimization checks<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awssupport/latest/user/cost-optimization-checks.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awssupport/latest/user/cost-optimization-checks.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– unused network components and additional data transfer costs.<\/li>\n<li>FinOps Foundation, <em>Unit Economics<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.finops.org/framework/capabilities/unit-economics/\">https://www.finops.org/framework/capabilities/unit-economics/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– relating costs to technically and commercially meaningful units of\nservice.<\/li>\n<li>FinOps Foundation, <em>Introduction to Cloud Unit Economics<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.finops.org/wg/introduction-cloud-unit-economics/\">https://www.finops.org/wg/introduction-cloud-unit-economics/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– definition, full costs and relationship between usage and value.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Not checked:<\/strong> Specific current VPS, GPU, CDN, API,\nelectricity or storage list prices for a particular provider or\ncontract. These would need to be established separately for a real\npurchase decision, with date, region and tariff.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M4-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M4-L5 – Possible software and cloud offerings of your own<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor’s / transition: Cloud operations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s / Master’s bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Initial problem: A technically possible solution is not yet\na product<\/h2>\n<p>After the lessons on servers, the cloud, databases, reliability and\ncosts, a new question arises: <strong>What could you offer using this\ninfrastructure that other people or businesses would voluntarily pay\nfor?<\/strong> A developer can operate a database, a web server or an AI\nworker. But this does not yet create a useful service. Only when an\noffering solves a real, sufficiently important problem and can be used\nreliably can it become a product.<\/p>\n<p>Three levels must be distinguished:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Technical concept:<\/strong> Analyse a file, generate a\nreport, synchronise data or process an AI job.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Deployment model:<\/strong> The program runs locally, on a\nrented server or on a managed cloud platform.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Business model:<\/strong> People pay once, monthly, per\nrequest, per user or for a managed service.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The same algorithms can be offered under different business models. A\nPDF analysis program could be sold as desktop software, rented as a web\ntool or made available as a programming interface for other\napplications. Conversely, the same monthly subscription can finance\neither a simple application or a complex cloud service. <strong>Software\nas a Service (SaaS)<\/strong> refers to the provision of software as a\nusable service; a subscription is a common but not a compulsory\nfeature.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>A user&#39;s specific problem\n             │\n             ▼\n       Useful function\n             │\n     ┌───────┴─────────┐\n     ▼                 ▼\nDesktop program    Online service\n     │                 │\nOne-off licence   Subscription / request / quota\n     │                 │\n     └───────┬─────────┘\n             ▼\n Support, updates, costs,\n demand and responsibility<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem does this distinction solve?<\/strong> It\nprevents hosting technology from being prematurely equated with a\nbusiness model. <strong>When is it not needed?<\/strong> Anyone\ndeveloping a tool exclusively for their own use or releasing it free of\ncharge does not have to invent a commercial model. Technical solutions\ncan be useful without any intention of generating revenue.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model 1: A small online tool as SaaS<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>SaaS offering<\/strong> provides an application over a\nnetwork. The provider operates and updates the software; customers use\nit, for example, in their browser. The main benefit to customers is not\nthat the software runs ‘in the cloud’, but that they can solve a problem\nwithout installing software themselves and often from several\ndevices.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000090_en.png\"\nalt=\"Schematic SaaS architecture with frontend, API, database and authorisation checks\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic SaaS architecture with\nfrontend, API, database and authorisation checks<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Imagine a service that reads large CSV files (<em>Comma-Separated\nValues<\/em>, simple tabular text files), checks them for errors and\ngenerates a report. The first technical implementation can be very\nstraightforward:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer in browser\n       │\n       ▼\nWeb application: upload file\n       │\n       ▼\nValidation and analysis module\n       │\n       ├── Display result\n       └── Download report<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If no accounts or permanent storage are required, this may already\nconstitute the complete product. Later, user accounts, recurring reports\nand separate customer areas could be added.\n<strong>Multi-tenancy<\/strong> means that multiple customers can use the\nsame application without seeing other customers’ confidential data. The\nAWS SaaS documentation addresses specific concepts such as tenant\nisolation, customer onboarding and user-related cost measurement;\nhowever, there is no single correct SaaS architecture for every\nproduct.<\/p>\n<p>A first version therefore need not consist of microservices,\nKubernetes or a database cluster. A single web server with a properly\nsecured program and a manageable database may suffice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> when users have a recurring problem, work on\nmultiple devices or do not want to install updates themselves.\n<strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> when a one-off local file conversion is\nentirely sufficient and no shared operation is needed.\n<strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> The provider takes responsibility for\navailability, account security, data deletion, troubleshooting and\npossibly ongoing customer support.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model 2: API-as-a-Service – Selling a function rather than\nan interface<\/h2>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong> stands for <em>Application Programming\nInterface<\/em>. <strong>API-as-a-Service<\/strong> means that customers\ncan call a function from their own software. They may not want to\noperate a dashboard or an additional website at all: their own system\nshould automatically accept data, process them and return a result.<\/p>\n<p>Example: A service analyses the structure of a file and reports\ntypical errors as machine-readable data.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer&#39;s software\n        │\n        ▼\nAPI request with data and access key\n        │\n        ▼\nAuthentication + authorisation check\n        │\n        ▼\nAnalysis function\n        │\n        ▼\nStructured response to customer software<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The interface needs a stable description of inputs, results and error\nstates. It must account for versions, access restrictions and protection\nagainst abuse. Months later, customer programs may still be calling the\nAPI automatically; an ill-considered change to its data format can break\nnumerous integrations. Documented versions and clearly communicated\nchanges can therefore be more important than an attractive\ninterface.<\/p>\n<p>Possible pricing arrangements include a fixed monthly request quota,\nusage-based charges or prepaid credit. For very computationally\nintensive jobs, billing must take account of the resources actually\nconsumed. A request taking 20 milliseconds may cost considerably less\nthan image processing that requires several minutes of GPU computing\ntime (<em>Graphics Processing Unit<\/em>).<\/p>\n<p>One security problem is <strong>unlimited resource requests<\/strong>.\nEven formally valid requests can overload a service through sheer\nvolume, large files or expensive computations. OWASP (<em>Open Worldwide\nApplication Security Project<\/em>) lists unrestricted resource\nconsumption as a distinct risk in its API security list. Countermeasures\ninclude access checks, quotas, file size limits, time limits, queues\nand, where appropriate, manual approval of costly actions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> for clearly definable functions that other\nprograms need repeatedly. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> for a tool whose\nuse requires substantial human interpretation.\n<strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> Interface changes and support for\nthird-party integrations create long-term obligations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model 3: Managed Automations – Recurring work as a managed\nservice<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong>Managed Automations<\/strong>, you sell not only a piece\nof software but a reliably completed task. For example, a small business\nmay need to consolidate data from several systems regularly, identify\nerrors and receive a weekly report.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Schedule or event\n          │\n          ▼\n   Automation service\n          │\n   ┌──────┴──────┐\n   ▼             ▼\nFetch data      Check inputs\n   └──────┬──────┘\n          ▼\n       Analysis\n          │\n          ▼\nReport + error message + log<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>scheduler<\/strong> (a time-based task planner), workers\n(background programs that execute tasks) and a small database may\nsuffice. Artificial intelligence is optional: a fixed rule can be more\nreliable, cheaper and easier to trace for invoice files, directories or\ntables. AI is more suitable where unstructured texts, classifications or\nfuzzy patterns need to be processed. Critical results require control\nmechanisms and, where appropriate, human approval.<\/p>\n<p>The customer often pays for the result, the set-up and ongoing\nsupport. This may have a higher perceived value than a licence alone. At\nthe same time, the responsibility for support increases: external data\nformats change, credentials expire, interfaces are modified and an error\ncan interrupt a necessary business task.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> for a small number of customers with\nrecurring, clearly described workflows. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> if\na simple guide or local script at the customer’s premises is sufficient.\n<strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> A highly customised service does not\nautomatically scale; each additional customer may require extra personal\neffort.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model 4: Hosted open-source software with added value<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Open source<\/strong> means that software is released under a\nlicence granting certain rights of use, inspection, modification and\nredistribution. Depending on the licence, you can operate a hosted\nservice based on that software and sell set-up, updates, maintenance or\nsupport, for example.<\/p>\n<p>The benefit to customers may arise from being able to use a powerful\nfree service without installing or administering it themselves.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Existing open-source project\n             │\n             ▼\nLegally permitted deployment\n             │\n             ▼\nHosting + set-up + updates\n             │\n             ▼\nCustomer receives managed access<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>specific software licence<\/strong> must be checked before\nany commercial use. ‘The source code is on the internet’ does not mean\n‘all forms of exploitation are permitted’. Conditions differ\nconsiderably between permissive licences, traditional copyleft licences\nand licences with network provisions. In particular, the GNU Affero\nGeneral Public License, version 3 (<strong>AGPLv3<\/strong>), includes\nobligations under certain conditions to offer users the corresponding\nsource code of a modified version when they interact with it over a\nnetwork. This does not imply either that every open-source project has\nthis rule or that hosting in general is prohibited.<\/p>\n<p>Trademark rights, images, bundled datasets and licences for\nextensions may also require separate consideration.\n<strong>Useful<\/strong> for a functioning project that can legally be\nused and has a genuine maintenance requirement.\n<strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> if customers can easily operate the\noffering themselves or there is no demand for support.\n<strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> You assume responsibility for software\nwhose technical development you do not fully control.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model 5: Data analysis, monitoring, media and AI\nworkflows<\/h2>\n<p>A number of offerings combine the models discussed above. Their\npractical benefit is what matters, not their classification into a\nmarketing category.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Offering idea<\/th>\n<th>Practical problem<\/th>\n<th>Possible small starting point<\/th>\n<th>Typical cost driver<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Data validation<\/td>\n<td>Faulty tables or imports<\/td>\n<td>Check a file and generate a report<\/td>\n<td>Computing time, support for unusual\nformats<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring<\/td>\n<td>Failures or changes noticed too late<\/td>\n<td>Check a few sources regularly<\/td>\n<td>Query frequency, notifications<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Media processing<\/td>\n<td>Convert many images/videos\nconsistently<\/td>\n<td>One file type, one processing profile<\/td>\n<td>Storage, data traffic, CPU/GPU<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI text analysis<\/td>\n<td>Classify or summarise free text<\/td>\n<td>One clearly defined document type<\/td>\n<td>API requests, model evaluation, data\nprotection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI image workflow<\/td>\n<td>Repeatable generation or editing<\/td>\n<td>One standardised job type<\/td>\n<td>GPU time, failed attempts, file\nstorage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Consider an image processing service. Customers upload images and\nchoose a defined processing operation. A web server accepts the job; a\nworker performs the computationally intensive work and makes the result\navailable for retrieval.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser / API\n      │\n      ▼\nJob submission with size limit\n      │\n      ▼\nJob database + queue\n      │\n      ▼\nCPU/GPU worker\n      │\n      ▼\nResult file with limited retention\n      │\n      ▼\nStatus and download for authorised customer<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The bottleneck may be computation time, GPU availability or outgoing\ndata traffic. Questions also arise concerning upload rights, permitted\ncontent, retention periods, retries, data protection, manipulation and\nthe handling of faulty results. A local AI computer can be economically\nsensible for your own production, but that alone does not make it\nsuitable as a public service: users expect availability, protection of\ntheir files and reliable processing times.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> for well-defined processes with repeatable\nresults. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> for a service that has to\ninterpret every job individually and has no clear quality standard.\n<strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> AI jobs can take an unpredictably long\ntime, fail or produce results that need checking before further use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model 6: Desktop licence with optional cloud\nsynchronisation<\/h2>\n<p>Not every application needs to become a web application. Locally\ninstalled desktop software can still have many advantages: data remain\non your own computer, the interface responds without an internet\nconnection and the basic functions incur no ongoing server costs.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>hybrid architecture<\/strong> combines this local strength\nwith individual optional online functions. Possible features include\nlicensed software, optional device synchronisation, encrypted backups or\na common user account for several computers.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Desktop application\n    │\n    ├── Local database (primary operation)\n    ├── Local processing (offline possible)\n    │\n    └── Optional sync service\n                 │\n                 ▼\n           Remote storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Synchronisation, however, is a technical task in its own right. What\nhappens if the same file or record is modified simultaneously on two\ndevices? This requires rules for conflicts and, where appropriate,\nversions, timestamps or synchronisation at record level. It must also be\nclear whether the remote storage merely holds additional copies or\nserves as the authoritative data source.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>one-off licence<\/strong> and an <strong>additional cloud\nsubscription<\/strong> can be separate products. Such a model may suit\nusers who want to remain independent but would pay for particular\nconvenience features.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> for data-intensive local applications with\nonly occasional synchronisation needs. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> when\nseveral users must work simultaneously on shared data and already need a\npurely server-side application. <strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> Windows,\nLinux or other desktop versions, offline problems and cloud components\nmay all need to be maintained in parallel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Billing: What exactly does a customer pay for?<\/h2>\n<p>The price should match the perceived benefit and the technical cost\nstructure. Typical models include:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th>What is paid for?<\/th>\n<th>Suitable when …<\/th>\n<th>Problem<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One-off purchase<\/td>\n<td>A permanently usable software\nversion/licence<\/td>\n<td>Product works mostly locally<\/td>\n<td>Subsequent updates and support need\nfunding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subscription<\/td>\n<td>Recurring access to the service<\/td>\n<td>Continuous benefit is provided<\/td>\n<td>Users expect ongoing availability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per user<\/td>\n<td>Number of authorised people<\/td>\n<td>Collaboration is the focus<\/td>\n<td>Shared accounts and roles become\nrelevant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per request/job<\/td>\n<td>Actual usage<\/td>\n<td>Costs rise roughly with usage<\/td>\n<td>Abuse and unexpected costs are\npossible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Credit package<\/td>\n<td>Prepaid units of usage<\/td>\n<td>Jobs are computationally intensive<\/td>\n<td>Remaining credit, refunds and accounting\nneed clarity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support contract<\/td>\n<td>Set-up, monitoring and assistance<\/td>\n<td>Customers buy results and\navailability<\/td>\n<td>Personal effort limits scaling<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>freemium model<\/strong> offers a free basic version and\npaid additional features. This can make access easier, but it is not\nself-sustaining by default: free users can consume computing power, data\ntraffic and support. Free quotas must therefore be limited just as paid\nones are.<\/p>\n<p>According to its official documentation, billing systems such as\nStripe provide subscriptions and usage-based methods with usage\nmetering. This is an example of a particular product, <strong>not a\ntechnical necessity<\/strong>, a recommendation to open an account or\nevidence of the current conditions of a particular plan. A payment\nprovider does not automatically take over product logic, access control,\ntax obligations and error handling in your own system.<\/p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish two truths: <strong>‘A payment\ntransaction has been confirmed’<\/strong> and <strong>‘The customer is\nentitled to use a particular service.’<\/strong> The application must\nrecord the appropriate entitlement after verified payment events;\nrepeated notifications must not credit the same service more than once.\nThis requires unique event identifiers, traceable records and correction\nprocedures (see M3-L5).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The actual assessment of a product idea<\/h2>\n<p>A technical idea becomes economically interesting only when several\nquestions align. As a guide, you can use a conceptual assessment\nframework – <strong>not as homework<\/strong>, but as a model of how a\ndeveloper evaluates possibilities.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"91-wer-braucht-die-loesung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Who needs the solution?<\/h3>\n<p>‘Everyone who has data’ is not a precise target group. ‘Small\nbusinesses that receive supplier files in an unsuitable tabular format\nevery month’ describes a more specific problem. The more clearly you\nidentify the user and their situation, the easier it is to determine an\nappropriate minimal offering.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"92-welches-problem-loest-sie-messbar\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What problem does it solve\nmeasurably?<\/h3>\n<p>A tool can save time, prevent errors, support a statutory\ndocumentation requirement or improve the quality of a recurring output.\nWhat matters is whether the advantage over existing solutions is\nrecognisable. ‘Uses artificial intelligence’ is initially only a\ntechnical property, not a customer benefit in its own right.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"93-was-ist-die-kleinste-glaubwuerdige-version\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is the\nsmallest credible version?<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>Minimum Viable Product (MVP)<\/strong> is a minimal,\ngenuinely usable product with which the central benefit can be tested.\nIt is not simply ‘something unfinished’: security and correct results\nmust not be missing. A data validator could, for example, process a\nsingle frequently occurring file type safely instead of already\nsupporting 40 formats unreliably.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"94-warum-sollte-jemand-bezahlen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why should someone pay?<\/h3>\n<p>Willingness to pay does not follow from technically interesting\nfunctions alone. Customers may already use a free alternative, encounter\nthe problem only rarely or be unable to assess the benefit sufficiently.\nA service is sustainable only if its revenue justifies its operation\ngiven realistic usage and support requirements. Extensive technical\nscalability proves neither demand nor economic viability.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"95-was-bleibt-auch-bei-wenigen-kunden-zu-tun\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What remains to be\ndone even with few customers?<\/h3>\n<p>Domains, hosting, updates, backups, monitoring, troubleshooting,\nlicensing questions, enquiries, invoices and data protection often\nbecome relevant with the first paying customer. A system with few paying\nusers can therefore require more support than expected.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model calculation: Revenue is not profit<\/h2>\n<p>The following example is <strong>entirely fictional<\/strong>. It\ndescribes neither market prices, actual service costs nor profit\nprospects.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose 40 customers use a small online tool at 15 euros per month\neach:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item per month<\/th>\n<th>Example amount<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Euro<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\times15\\,\\text{Euro}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncustomer revenue<\/td>\n<td>600 euros<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variable processing costs:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Euro<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\times2\\,\\text{Euro}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>−80 euros<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server, backups and basic technical\ncosts<\/td>\n<td>−90 euros<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payment processing as a model flat\nfee<\/td>\n<td>−20 euros<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Calculated remainder before labour\nand further costs<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>410 euros<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This remainder is <strong>not the profit<\/strong>. If we additionally\nassume five hours of support and maintenance at a purely hypothetical\nvalue of 30 euros per hour, the remaining amount falls to 260 euros.\nTaxes, possible refunds, bookkeeping, development and other costs have\nnot yet been accounted for. If more time is required or there are fewer\ncustomers, the service can become unprofitable.<\/p>\n<p>A useful concept is <strong>unit economics<\/strong>: how much does\none successfully delivered service cost, including an appropriate share\nof common costs? In an AI service, a high failure rate can significantly\nchange this figure. In a managed-service offering, by contrast, the time\nspent on personal support may dominate.<\/p>\n<p>The number of customers alone is also insufficient for an assessment.\nActual usage, cancellations, necessary support and the costs of\nacquiring new customers matter, among other things. A small service may\nbe technically able to grow tenfold at any time and still become\neconomically worse if jobs cost more than the revenue they generate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security, data protection, contracts and licences<\/h2>\n<p>As soon as other people use a service, further responsibilities\narise. The most important depend on the specific offering:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security:<\/strong> User accounts, separate customer data,\nfile uploads, access keys, protection against abuse, security updates\nand recoverable backups.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Data protection:<\/strong> Processing personal data,\ndeletion, retention, access rights and the role of controller or\nprocessor under the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). The\nclassification depends on the actual decisions about the purposes and\nmeans of processing, not the marketing term ‘cloud’.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Contractual and consumer matters:<\/strong> A clear\ndescription of the service, cancellation, rights of withdrawal where\napplicable, invoices, support and promised availability. The applicable\nobligations depend on the type of customer, the country and the specific\ncontractual arrangements, among other factors.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Taxes:<\/strong> Cross-border billing for digital services\ncan raise VAT questions. The EU <strong>One Stop Shop (OSS)<\/strong>\nprocedure can simplify certain cross-border sales to consumers under its\nstatutory conditions; it is not a blanket tax exemption.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Licences and content:<\/strong> Check rights to source code,\nmodels, training data, images, music, fonts and additional libraries\nseparately.<\/li>\n<li><strong>AI-specific consequences:<\/strong> Consider risks arising\nfrom incorrect results, confidential inputs, manipulated instructions\nand unchecked automatic actions. Safety-relevant decisions require\nappropriate oversight.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Even a very small provider can process personal data and be subject\nto corresponding obligations. Conversely, not all requirements are\nidentical for every product. The points listed here are <strong>not a\nsubstitute for legally binding advice<\/strong>. Current statutory\nobligations must be checked separately for a specific undertaking.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Six ideas as a technical decision-making aid<\/h2>\n<p>The following table assesses <strong>spaces of possibility<\/strong>,\nnot real market opportunities. No demand or eventual revenue is assumed\nfor any idea.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Who might benefit?<\/th>\n<th>Minimal technical core<\/th>\n<th>Main burden<\/th>\n<th>When not useful?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tabular data validator as SaaS<\/td>\n<td>People with recurring import errors<\/td>\n<td>Upload, validation, report<\/td>\n<td>Data protection, format support<\/td>\n<td>Rare one-off use is sufficiently\nlocal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis API<\/td>\n<td>Developers with a fixed analysis need<\/td>\n<td>Endpoint, authentication, clear\nresponse<\/td>\n<td>Interface maintenance, quotas<\/td>\n<td>No automated integration needed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed report automation<\/td>\n<td>Small teams with routine reports<\/td>\n<td>Data import, schedule, result<\/td>\n<td>External systems and personal support<\/td>\n<td>Each job remains individual<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hosted open-source tool<\/td>\n<td>Users without their own IT support<\/td>\n<td>Lawful hosting, updates, help<\/td>\n<td>Operating third-party software,\nlicences<\/td>\n<td>Self-hosting is already effortless<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI media processing<\/td>\n<td>Teams with recurring identical media\njobs<\/td>\n<td>Job submission, worker, result file<\/td>\n<td>GPU costs, result quality<\/td>\n<td>No standardisable process<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Desktop tool with optional sync<\/td>\n<td>Individual users with several devices<\/td>\n<td>Local database and voluntary\nsynchronisation<\/td>\n<td>Sync conflicts, two product\ncomponents<\/td>\n<td>Purely local or purely web-based\nsuffices<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For any idea, appropriate building blocks can be selected from the\nprevious lessons: <strong>M2-L1–M2-L3<\/strong> for deployment,\n<strong>M2-L4–M2-L5<\/strong> for containers and scaling,\n<strong>M3-L1–M3-L6<\/strong> for data and data security,\n<strong>M4-L1–M4-L2<\/strong> for jobs and reliable operation, and\n<strong>M4-L3–M4-L4<\/strong> for security and costs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a standalone application to a service – without\npremature complexity<\/h2>\n<p>A sensible starting point can be technically very simple:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Version 1: Local tool\n    File → Program → Result\n\nVersion 2: Simple online service\n    Browser → Web server/application → Database\n\nVersion 3: Several concurrent jobs\n    Browser/API → Application → Queue → Worker\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n                            Result store\n\nVersion 4: Extension according to need\n    additional workers / database replicas /\n    separate customer areas – only as needed<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This sequence <strong>is not a mandatory development path<\/strong>.\nMany products sensibly remain at version 1 or 2 for years. Your own VPS\n(<em>Virtual Private Server<\/em>), conventional hosting or a simple\nmanaged platform may suffice. Kubernetes, large database clusters and\nmultiple cloud regions are not signs of greater business maturity if\nthey do not solve a real problem.<\/p>\n<p>For AI offerings, it is also worth distinguishing between\n<strong>model<\/strong>, <strong>application<\/strong> and\n<strong>service<\/strong>. A publicly available language or image model\nis not yet a product of your own. Clearly defined processing, traceable\nquality checks, appropriate controls and reliable execution can create\nthe actual benefit. The service should not rely on capabilities that the\nmodel cannot deliver reliably.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to the AI section<\/h2>\n<p>The server and cloud technology learnt in this course opens up a wide\nrange of technical possibilities: local software, paid web tools, APIs,\nmanaged automation, hosted open-source projects, data-intensive services\nand hybrid desktop/cloud products. Not every one of these possibilities\nrequires a cloud provider, a large cluster of servers or artificial\nintelligence.<\/p>\n<p>The crucial questions remain: <strong>Who benefits from the service?\nWhat is the smallest technically reliable scope? Why would someone be\nwilling to pay for it? What obligations and real costs result from\nit?<\/strong> The ability to scale a system tells us nothing about\nwhether there is sufficient demand.<\/p>\n<p>This completes course section <strong>D – Cloud operations and\neconomic application<\/strong>. The next section, <strong>E – Artificial\nintelligence and robotics<\/strong>, begins with the historical ideas and\nexplores how formal neuron models developed into modern neural networks,\ntransformers, large language models, agents and eventually systems with\nperception and action.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Date of the technical source review: 04.11.2026.<\/strong> The\nfollowing sources substantiate technical and legal foundations. The\nproduct examples and fictional figures are teaching models, not\nstatements about real prices, actual demand or profit prospects.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>AWS: <em>Well-Architected SaaS Lens – Definitions<\/em> (tenants,\nisolation, pool/silo models). <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/saas-lens/definitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/saas-lens/definitions.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>AWS: <em>SaaS Lens – General Design Principles<\/em> (no universal\nSaaS pattern; onboarding and tenant-related measurement). <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/saas-lens/general-design-principles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/saas-lens/general-design-principles.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Stripe: <em>Billing Features<\/em> (subscriptions and usage-based\nbilling as a concrete product example). <a\nhref=\"https://stripe.com/billing/features\">https://stripe.com/billing/features<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>OWASP: <em>API Security Top 10 – 2023<\/em> (resource abuse, access\ncontrol and API risks). <a\nhref=\"https://api-security.owasp.org/\">https://api-security.owasp.org/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>GNU Project: <em>GNU Affero General Public License Version 3<\/em>,\nespecially section 13 (network interaction and corresponding source code\nfor certain modified programs). <a\nhref=\"https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.de.html\">https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.de.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Commission: <em>Application of the GDPR<\/em>\n(controllers/processors, particular considerations for small\nbusinesses). <a\nhref=\"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/application-gdpr_en\">https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/application-gdpr_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Data Protection Board (EDPB): <em>Data controller or data\nprocessor<\/em> (roles and obligations). <a\nhref=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/learn-the-basics/data-controller-or-data-processor_en\">https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/learn-the-basics/data-controller-or-data-processor_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>European Union, Your Europe: <em>Mehrwertsteuer – One Stop Shop<\/em>\n(cross-border VAT administration under certain conditions). <a\nhref=\"https://europa.eu/youreurope/business/finance-and-tax/vat/one-stop-shop/index_de.htm\">https://europa.eu/youreurope/business/finance-and-tax/vat/one-stop-shop/index_de.htm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>FinOps Foundation: <em>Unit Economics<\/em> (cost per business unit).\n<a\nhref=\"https://www.finops.org/framework/capabilities/unit-economics/\">https://www.finops.org/framework/capabilities/unit-economics/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Scope:<\/strong> This lesson teaches fundamental technical and\neconomic principles, not individual business, tax, licensing or legal\nadvice.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
